# Authored tile-by-tile examples; all organizations, numbers, and policies below are hypothetical.
A={}
def add(i,example,contrast,note,level='LOW',mode='slides_with_signaling',reason=None):
 A[i]=(example,contrast,note,level,mode,reason)
add(0,'''Imagine an operations manager saying, “We need a new order system.” That names a possible solution, but it leaves the underlying problem unclear. For this example, suppose dispatch staff at the North branch cannot find the latest delivery instructions, and repeated clarification calls delay dispatch. Those details let you frame a problem without deciding what to buy.

A bounded statement could read: “North-branch dispatch staff cannot reliably find the latest delivery instructions when preparing an order, causing repeated clarification calls and delayed dispatch.” It identifies who is affected, what is wrong, why it matters, and where it occurs. The sentence does not promise that a new system is the right response.

Now write the boundary beside it: “Included: finding and communicating the latest delivery instructions during North-branch dispatch preparation. Excluded: changing delivery prices, replacing vehicles, or changing other branches' operations.” These are invented boundaries for this exercise, not general exclusions for every BA assignment. If a stakeholder asks to redesign vehicle purchasing, compare that request with the stated included and excluded work. The useful output is a problem statement and an explicit boundary, not a list of features disguised as a problem.''','''“Implement an app” is not the same as clarifying the problem. Reword it around the affected people and difficulty before deciding on a solution. A concise problem statement also does not replace the included and excluded work: both need to be visible.''','Keep solution requirements and document templates for later; establish the problem and boundary first.')
add(1,'''Suppose dispatch staff describe delivery instructions as “hard to use.” You need to learn what happens during preparation, not persuade them that a particular replacement is desirable. Set the interview aim as understanding the expected outcome, current operating conditions, and constraints on accessing instructions.

Prepare a script with questions such as “Describe how you find the instructions for an order,” “What information do you need before dispatch?” and “What happens when that information is unclear?” A structured interview follows that script; a flexibly ordered guide would instead be semistructured. Keep each question concise and leave room for the participant’s own account.

When a participant says, “It should be quick,” ask what concrete outcome they expect, under which conditions, and what observable or measurable result would show that the need is met. For this hypothetical interview, they might specify finding the current instructions within 30 seconds during dispatch preparation. Record that elicited need in the shared requirements document and link it to the interview context, including the conditions discussed. The numerical threshold is example stakeholder input, not a universal requirement. The finished record should preserve both the stated need and the context that supports it, rather than only your summary of “make it faster.”''','''Open-ended does not mean directionless: the questions still serve defined learning aims. Conversely, asking “Would a new mobile screen fix this?” suggests an answer instead of letting the participant describe their experience. A vague answer calls for concrete follow-up, not an invented requirement.''','Do not turn this into workshop facilitation or survey design; teach script, open questions, probing, and contextual recording first.')
add(2,'''Imagine dispatch, sales, and customer-support representatives disagreeing about which delivery instructions must be available before dispatch. A workshop brings them together for goal-oriented activities and discussion. Define its outcome as a shared account of the information needed, with any unresolved questions recorded.

Build the agenda from that goal. Questions might include “What must dispatch know?” and “Which team supplies each piece of information?” Choose activities that address those questions, such as having participants describe an order and then discuss the required information together. In this hypothetical agenda, allocate ten minutes to the goal, twenty to the order exercise, twenty to discussion, and ten to consolidating outputs. Prepare a shared capture sheet before the session so contributed requirements and questions have a place to go.

During discussion, guide contributions back to the agenda’s questions. A lengthy discussion of vehicle purchasing does not answer the agreed information question, so return to the intended outcome. Afterward, analyze the captured material and share the resulting requirements and unresolved questions with participants. The workshop is not finished merely because its time has elapsed: the collected outputs still need analysis and sharing. The example timings and activity are choices for this situation, not a mandatory workshop format.''','''A workshop is not simply several people receiving the same presentation. Its activities and discussion are directed at shared outcomes. A detailed agenda helps only when its questions and activities actually support the agreed goals.''','Keep named workshop methodologies short; avoid implying one mandatory agenda sequence.','MEDIUM','talking_head_with_visual_support','A facilitated session demonstration can show how activities and discussion are guided toward a shared outcome; the text supplies the planning logic.')
add(3,'''Suppose an order-service team wants to understand preferences across several branches. The research aim is to compare how important respondents consider finding delivery instructions quickly. A survey fits that attitudinal question better than a request for a detailed account of everything someone actually does during dispatch.

An example question could be “How important is access to the latest delivery instructions when preparing dispatch?” with clearly stated response options. Another could ask how satisfied the respondent is with current access. Keep questions tied to the stated research aim. Asking about unrelated office preferences would collect information without answering the requirements question.

Compare “How satisfied are you with the current instruction lookup?” with “How much do you love our improved lookup?” The second pushes toward a favorable answer. Also check whether respondents can accurately answer what is being asked: a person who does not prepare dispatch may not be able to describe that experience. In this exercise, the survey’s output is quantified attitudes and preferences about instruction access. It is not a reconstruction of the actual sequence of dispatch work. Maintain that distinction when explaining the results to stakeholders so the requirements discussion does not treat a preference score as a detailed behavioral account.''','''A large set of responses does not change the kind of evidence collected. Stated attitudes remain attitudes; they are not detailed observations of behavior. Easy-to-ask questions also do not belong automatically: each should serve the research aims.''','Do not introduce sampling statistics or claim surveys directly establish actual behavior.')
add(4,'''Imagine being given an old dispatch manual and a contract for an order-service change. The manual says a supervisor approves amended delivery instructions; the contract says the service must retain an instruction record. These are example statements from existing material, not requirements you have independently confirmed.

Review the material systematically and record what each statement contributes. Put the approval requirement and record-retention constraint in the shared requirements document, linking each to the supporting manual or contract passage. If the manual assumes that only one branch processes an order, record that assumption too. If the contract does not identify who may amend instructions, record an open question rather than filling the gap with your own preference.

Then confirm the documented requirements with the relevant stakeholders before treating them as work to execute. For example, ask whether the supervisor approval rule is still intended and which scope it covers. A stakeholder might confirm it, request a change, or leave a question unresolved; preserve the resulting understanding in the shared record. The practical skill is extracting requirements, assumptions, constraints, and questions with their context intact. Merely copying paragraphs into a new file leaves the learner without a clear account of what has been found and what still needs confirmation.''','''Existing text is an elicitation channel, not automatic confirmation that every old statement should be implemented now. Keep the supporting context and confirm requirements with stakeholders; do not silently turn an unresolved passage into an agreed requirement.''','Avoid contractual or regulatory interpretation; use invented document statements only.')
add(5,'''Suppose a dispatcher says, “I open the order and send it to the driver.” Observe the dispatcher doing the work in its actual context, then ask about the actions you saw. In this example, you observe the dispatcher open the order, consult a separate instruction sheet, call sales to clarify an address, and then send the order to the driver.

Record the performed tasks and their sequence. You can describe the goal as preparing an order for dispatch and identify subtasks such as finding instructions and resolving uncertainty. Ask questions about those observed steps: “What information were you checking on the separate sheet?” or “What made the clarification call necessary?” These questions follow the observation instead of replacing it with a guessed explanation.

Keep the stated process alongside the observed process. The statement omitted the separate-sheet lookup and clarification call; log that discrepancy with notes of the observed actions that support it. Do not change the observation record to make the two accounts agree. The comparison helps you understand how this work was actually performed in the observed situation. It does not establish that every dispatcher always follows the same sequence. The useful result is a supported account of tasks and discrepancies that can inform requirements, not a judgment about whether the participant gave a good interview answer.''','''Observation is more than asking someone what they normally do. It examines work as performed and then uses questions to understand what was seen. A difference between an account and observed steps should be documented with action notes, not replaced by speculation about motive.''','Keep process notation for later; teach task records and stated-versus-observed comparison.','HIGH','talking_head_with_visual_support','A work-context demonstration can make the contrast between a stated process and observed subtasks visible.')
add(6,'''Imagine stakeholders agreeing that dispatch staff need “a fast instruction lookup.” Agreement confirms an intended need, but the wording still leaves several questions: what counts as fast, under what conditions, and how will anyone determine whether the requirement has been met?

Present the documented goal, scope, requirements, assumptions, and open questions to stakeholders, capturing agreement or requested changes. That addresses validation of the intended business need. Separately verify the written requirement. Check completeness against the documented goals, success metrics, scope, categories, assumptions, and constraints. If the operating conditions are missing, record an open question rather than assuming them.

For ambiguity, ask whether stakeholders interpret “fast” the same way. In this hypothetical case, they clarify the requirement as “During normal dispatch preparation, the current instructions for a selected order are displayed within two seconds.” The condition and threshold are example decisions made by these stakeholders. For testability, identify an executable test: select an order in the stated conditions, measure the time until its current instructions appear, and compare that time with two seconds. The pass/fail result is objective; “it felt fast” is not.

The review therefore has two different outputs: agreement about the intended need, and wording that has been checked for completeness, ambiguity, and testability. Keep unresolved questions visible until the missing information is resolved.''','''Approval does not by itself prove that a sentence is unambiguous or testable. Nor does a precise threshold prove the requirement serves the intended business need. Validation and verification address different questions, so one cannot stand in for the other.''','Keep the validation/verification distinction explicit; do not invent mandatory test tooling.')
add(7,'''Suppose stakeholders struggle to explain what they need on a delivery-instruction screen. Instead of implementing the screen, draw a simple representation for discussion. A low-fidelity prototype can be inexpensive and relatively simple while still letting stakeholders explore a design.

For this example, sketch a page with an order identifier at the top, a large area for current instructions, and controls labeled “Select order” and “Confirm instructions.” These labels and areas are invented design choices for the exercise. Leave out detailed colors, typography, and visual styling. The wireframe’s job here is to represent the main content areas and controls, not to establish the final appearance.

Use the sketch to gather feedback before investing in a fully implemented version. A dispatcher might point to the instruction area and ask for the last amendment to be visible; another might explain that the confirmation control does not match their intended task. Record what the feedback reveals rather than treating every drawn control as an agreed requirement. The representation gives stakeholders something concrete to respond to while the design is still being explored. After discussion, the useful output is feedback about the design and needs it surfaces, not a claim that the screen is complete simply because all its boxes have labels.''','''A wireframe need not look like a finished interface. Adding detailed styling is not what makes this low-fidelity elicitation useful. Also distinguish the proposed design from the feedback collected about it: a sketched control is not automatically an agreed requirement.''','Keep implementation and detailed UI design out of scope.','HIGH','whiteboard_drawing','Drawing and revising a simple screen makes low-fidelity content areas, controls, and feedback tangible.')
add(8,'''Imagine the team has agreed on the problem “Dispatch staff cannot find current delivery instructions.” The brainstorming goal is to generate alternative ideas for addressing that problem, not to decide immediately which idea wins.

State the problem and goal to the participants. For this hypothetical session, choose a facilitator, set a fifteen-minute limit, and prepare a place to record ideas. Contributors suggest a single instruction summary, a clearer amendment label, or a revised handoff between sales and dispatch. Record those contributed ideas so the discussion does not depend on remembering who said what.

Keep generation separate from judgment. If a participant responds to the first suggestion with “That will never work,” the facilitator can return the session to generating alternatives and leave evaluation for a separate discussion. This does not mean every idea must later be selected. It means the current activity is exploration, so an idea is recorded before it is judged.

At the end, the result is a set of alternatives connected to the same shared problem. The example ideas are possibilities, not recommended solutions. The distinction matters for a BA: a session aimed at generating ideas should not be described as if it had already established requirements or chosen an implementation. The facilitator, limit, and record provide a concrete frame for that exploration.''','''Separating generation from judgment is not the same as promising to implement every suggestion. Brainstorming produces alternatives for a shared problem. A session that immediately debates each contribution is mixing the two activities rather than preserving the stated separation.''','Avoid adding evaluation methods not taught by this concept.')
add(9,'''Suppose an order-service team wants to hear how dispatch staff feel about a proposed instruction summary. A focus group is a moderated discussion of attitudes and opinions. Prepare a moderator script around specific issues: whether the summary feels clear, which information participants consider important, and where their preferences differ.

In the hypothetical discussion, one participant says the latest amendment should be most prominent; another prefers the complete instruction history. Record both opinions and the disagreement. Invite quieter participants to contribute and keep the conversation on the script’s issues. If one participant takes over, guide the discussion so their view does not become the only view captured.

The resulting record might say, “Participants disagreed about showing a summary versus a complete history,” with the preferences they expressed. It should not say, “Dispatchers use summaries during actual work,” because that behavior was not established by this discussion. The group’s stated preferences can inform requirements exploration without being relabeled as observation.

For the BA, the practical output is a supported account of attitudes, including disagreement. The moderator’s script keeps the information goal visible, while balanced participation lets more than one perspective be expressed. The invented design choices in this example are prompts for discussion, not a finding that either choice is the correct solution.''','''A focus group does not become evidence of actual product use merely because participants describe what they prefer. Keep stated opinions and disagreements as such. Moderating participation also does not require erasing disagreement to make the record look unanimous.''','Do not substitute group opinion for observation.','MEDIUM','talking_head_with_visual_support','A moderated discussion example can show inviting contributions and handling dominance while preserving differing opinions.')
add(10,'''Imagine eliciting requirements for delivery-instruction access. A stakeholder reports that an external address feed may not be ready when needed. Record that known risk together with the blocker or dependency information supplied during elicitation, rather than leaving it outside the requirements discussion.

Assess its likelihood and impact. For this hypothetical matrix, the team labels likelihood low, medium, or high and impact low, medium, or high. Suppose the address-feed risk is assessed as medium likelihood and high impact. Place it at that intersection and use the matrix’s agreed risk-level labels to determine its level. The category boundaries are choices for this example; the concept does not supply a universal numerical formula or universal label for that cell.

A second risk might have high likelihood but low impact. Locate it separately rather than assuming frequency and consequence are the same thing. The matrix evaluates both dimensions, so your record should preserve the two assessments as well as the resulting level.

The useful requirements output is a list of identified risks, blockers, and dependencies with reasoned likelihood and impact assessments. It is not a promise that a risk will occur. Nor is merely drawing a grid enough: the assessment and the matrix position must be connected to each identified risk.''','''Likelihood is not impact. A frequently occurring inconvenience and a less likely severe disruption can occupy different matrix positions. Do not invent a universal score from category words; use the risk-level interpretation of the matrix being used.''','Keep mitigation frameworks and unsupported scoring formulas out of this explanation.','HIGH','diagram_animation','Placing risks on two independent axes makes likelihood, impact, and the resulting matrix position clear.')
add(11,'''Suppose the business wants fewer dispatch delays caused by unclear instructions. That broader outcome is a business requirement; it is not yet a detailed description of every action a system must perform.

Organize a shared requirements document around the effort. In this hypothetical document, the summary describes the instruction-access problem, the goal is fewer clarification delays, and a success metric is a stated target for dispatch preparation time. The scope includes the North branch and excludes vehicle purchasing. Record the elicited requirements, along with assumptions such as availability of the current instruction record and constraints such as a required approval. These example entries illustrate the shared structure; they do not prescribe the same content for every initiative.

Review the organized requirements with approvers to agree what will be delivered. If an approver questions the branch boundary or success target, capture the issue rather than treating the document’s existence as agreement. The document gathers the business outcome and supporting delivery context in one place so the review has something specific to consider.

A reader should be able to distinguish the reason for the work from the items expected within its scope. For example, “reduce clarification delays” states the outcome, while the requirement entries describe the agreed needs supporting it. Keep that distinction visible instead of replacing the business goal with a long feature list.''','''A business requirements document is not made complete by a title or a feature list. The broad outcome and the supporting context need to be clear, and the elicited requirements still need review with approvers. The listed sections are possible contents, not a closed mandatory template.''','Preserve “can contain”; keep functional specification detail for its own concept.')
add(12,'''Imagine the business outcome is fewer dispatch delays. A functional requirement supporting that outcome might be “Display the current delivery instructions when a dispatcher selects an order.” This describes what the deliverable must do, rather than restating the outcome or saying how quickly it should operate.

Expand it into a hypothetical specification entry. The initiating event is selecting an order; the input is the selected order identifier. The expected response is to retrieve and display that order’s current instructions, and the output is the displayed instruction text. A precondition could be that the order exists; a postcondition could be that its current instructions are visible. For an unknown identifier, the example exception behavior is to display a clear not-found result instead of instruction text. These are example requirements, not claims about an existing product.

The detailed entry now gives a reader the behavior for both the ordinary situation and the relevant exception. Keep the broader business outcome in its own place and document performance expectations separately. “Reduce delays” explains why the work matters, but it does not tell the reader what happens when an identifier is selected.

The practical skill is moving from a vague function label such as “instruction access” to an explicit event, inputs, response, outputs, and applicable conditions. Do not invent preconditions or exceptions silently; in real elicitation those details need to be established for the requirement.''','''A functional specification is not a list of business aspirations. Nor does “fast lookup” fully describe the function. Specify the expected behavior and applicable conditions separately from the broader outcome and the way the solution should perform.''','Do not introduce implementation APIs, database commands, or technical design.')
add(13,'''Suppose a dispatcher needs current instructions before releasing an order. An example story is “As a dispatcher, I want to see the current delivery instructions for a selected order, so that I can prepare the correct dispatch.” The persona, need, and purpose are visible without turning the story into a technical design.

The story still needs specific conditions for acceptance. One hypothetical scenario is:
Given an existing order has current delivery instructions
When the dispatcher selects that order
Then the current instructions are displayed for the dispatcher.

Given establishes the initial context, When names the action, and Then states an observable result. Keep the lines in that order. A second scenario could address an unknown order:
Given an order identifier does not exist
When the dispatcher selects that identifier
Then a not-found result is displayed.

These conditions describe what must be satisfied for this story to be accepted in the example. They do not claim the two scenarios cover every need in a real system. Discuss the relevant conditions rather than assuming the story sentence supplies them all.

The BA’s work is to connect the user-perspective outcome with specific acceptance conditions. A reader should be able to see who benefits and then identify the result to check. “The feature works correctly” would not provide the same observable expectation.''','''The user-story sentence and acceptance criteria are related but not interchangeable. The sentence communicates the desired user outcome; the scenarios state specific conditions for acceptance. A Then line should describe an observable result, not merely say that the action was performed.''','Keep stories user-centered; do not introduce later refinement checklists yet.')
add(14,'''Imagine a dispatcher asking an order service for current instructions. In a use case, “dispatcher” is the actor role interacting with the subject; it is not the name of one particular employee. Another system could also act as an actor if it interacts with the subject.

For this hypothetical use case, state the valued result as obtaining current delivery instructions. Describe the basic interaction in natural language: the dispatcher identifies an order, the service finds its current instructions, and the service presents them. A relevant precondition is that the dispatcher has an order to identify. A postcondition for the successful interaction is that the current instructions have been presented.

Include relevant variations and exceptions. For example, if the identifier is unknown, describe the not-found response. If no instructions are recorded, describe the agreed response for that situation rather than leaving the basic interaction to imply success. The conditions and responses are invented here to show the specification’s structure, not existing tool behavior.

The useful output is an account of behavior that gives an actor or stakeholder an observable result of value, together with the relevant conditions and alternate behavior. A short label such as “view instructions” names an interaction but leaves its behavior unspecified. Conversely, the specification can remain in natural language; this concept does not require programming the interaction.''','''An actor is a role, not necessarily a human or a named employee. A use case also needs more than its successful path when variations or error behavior are relevant. Include those behaviors and conditions rather than letting a success-only description stand for every situation.''','Avoid unsupported use-case templates or UML symbols.')
add(15,'''Suppose requirement R-12 says a dispatcher must see current instructions for a selected order. Its originating business need is reducing clarification delays. A traceability matrix keeps that connection alongside links to the work that fulfills and checks the requirement.

Create a row for R-12 with columns for the originating need or source, related design artifact, and test-case identifiers. In this hypothetical record, link the source to Need-3, the design to Design-7, and the tests to Test-21 and Test-22. These labels stand for actual referenced artifacts in the exercise. A cell saying “some testing” is not the same as a reference to the test artifacts.

Now suppose R-12 changes to include an amended-instruction indicator. Follow its links to identify related design, development, and testing work that may need updating. The matrix helps you find affected work; the requirement change should not remain isolated in its own paragraph.

Review link coverage for the stage being considered. No source link leaves the requirement orphaned from its business justification. A missing design or test link is a coverage gap when that artifact is required at that stage. This qualification matters: do not demand a later-stage artifact merely because its column exists. The practical result is connected requirements and stage-appropriate evidence, not just a table filled with descriptions.''','''A matrix is not traceability merely because it has columns. Its entries must reference the supporting artifacts. Also distinguish a missing required link from a link to an artifact not yet required at the reviewed stage; the latter qualification should not be discarded.''','Do not imply every artifact must exist at every stage.','HIGH','diagram_animation','Following links from a changed requirement to source, design, and tests makes change impact and missing coverage visible.')
add(16,'''Imagine R-12 has been agreed for delivery, then a stakeholder proposes adding an amended-instruction indicator. Requirements life cycle management keeps that revision and its decision visible instead of silently replacing the current requirement.

Use an explicit customized workflow. In this example, draft means preparation or review, approved means agreed for delivery, change under review means a proposed revision awaiting a decision, and retired means no longer current. Record R-12’s current status and link it to current work-item status. A proposed revision is not the same as an approved replacement.

Record the affected requirement and change request, review the risks and expected outcomes, and record the approval decision and status. If approved and implemented, document the change and its results. Follow traceability links to identify and update related work. If the requirement is retired, make that transition explicit too rather than leaving an obsolete requirement looking current.

When the requirement lives on a Confluence page, modifying the page creates a new version. Record workflow status in the document, then use More options > Page History to inspect version numbers and compare the revision with the preceding version. Page versions show revisions; the documented workflow status shows whether a revision is draft, agreed, under review, or retired. Keep those two ideas separate.

The BA’s record should let a reader understand the current requirement, the proposed change, the decision, and the resulting work without guessing from whichever paragraph was edited last.''','''A newer page version does not by itself mean an approved requirement. Approval, revision review, and retirement are explicit workflow transitions. Likewise, editing requirement text without following its traceability links leaves the related work unaddressed.''','Treat workflow as customized, not a built-in universal Confluence status machine.','HIGH','annotated_ui','A status-and-version walkthrough can distinguish requirement decisions from page revisions and show comparison through Page History.')
add(17,'''Suppose a requirement says the service must display delivery instructions. That describes a function. A request that the display be “fast” describes a performance expectation, but its wording does not yet supply a measurable target.

For this hypothetical situation, stakeholders specify “Current delivery instructions are displayed within two seconds when a dispatcher selects an existing order during normal dispatch preparation.” The numerical target replaces the vague performance word. The function remains displaying instructions; the performance condition says how the solution should perform when doing so.

Compare the two statements side by side. “Display the instructions” answers what the deliverable must do. “Within two seconds under the stated conditions” answers a question about performance or operation. The two-second threshold is example stakeholder input, not a universal definition of acceptable lookup performance.

When documenting the requirement, preserve the concrete target instead of summarizing it back to “good performance.” The measurable expectation gives the requirements discussion something specific to agree about. In this example, a measured three-second display does not satisfy the stated two-second expectation even if a participant describes it as reasonably quick. That is the practical difference between a numerical target and a favorable adjective. Keep the treatment focused on measurable performance rather than introducing a catalog of quality attributes that this brief does not establish.''','''Here, nonfunctional distinguishes how the solution performs or operates from the functions it provides. “User-friendly and fast” is not a concrete numerical performance target. Do not confuse a positive description with a measurable expectation.''','Focus on the supported performance distinction; avoid unsupported taxonomies.')
add(18,'''Imagine mapping the people involved in changing delivery-instruction access. Dispatch staff use the deliverable; sales staff provide instruction input; an operations manager approves the change; a sponsor funds it. Customer-support staff may be affected by changes in clarification work. These are hypothetical roles, not a fixed stakeholder list for every initiative.

Record who uses, provides input to, approves, funds, or is affected by the deliverable. Classify their involvement: some are directly involved or affected, some indirectly affected or consulted, and some reviewers or approvers. Roles can overlap. For example, the operations manager might be directly affected by dispatch outcomes and also an approver. Do not force that person into just one category if both roles apply.

Record each stakeholder’s role and assess their interest and power over the work. In this example, a dispatcher may have strong interest in usable instructions but less decision power than the approving manager. The manager’s formal approval role does not tell you everything about their degree of interest, so keep the two assessments visible.

The output is more useful than a contact list: it explains why each person or group belongs in the analysis and how they relate to the deliverable. Begin with involvement and effect rather than assuming only senior people count as stakeholders. People whose work changes can belong even when they do not approve the budget.''','''A stakeholder is not only a sponsor or decision-maker. Interest, needed involvement, and being affected can all matter. Classification categories can overlap, so assigning one role should not erase another role the same person actually holds.''','Do not teach the matrix’s engagement quadrants before its own entry.')
add(19,'''Suppose the operations manager has high power over an instruction-access change and high interest in its outcome. Dispatch staff have high interest but lower power. A finance approver has high power but limited day-to-day interest, while an indirectly affected group has low power and low interest. These are example assessments, not assumptions about everyone with those titles.

Plot them on a grid with a low-to-high power axis and a separate low-to-high interest axis. The manager’s high/high position calls for close management. The high-power, low-interest approver should be kept satisfied. Dispatch staff in the low-power, high-interest position should be kept informed. The low/low group is monitored with less intensive engagement.

Read each position using both axes. Low power does not imply low interest, and high power does not automatically imply high interest. In the example, dispatch staff and the finance approver occupy different quadrants even though both matter to the initiative.

The matrix turns the assessed combination into an engagement approach. It does not identify stakeholders on its own or prescribe a universal message frequency. If you describe the dispatch group as needing information, point back to its assessed low-power/high-interest position rather than simply claiming all users belong there. The practical skill is matching the action to the plotted combination while keeping the assessments explicit.''','''Do not collapse power and interest into one importance label. “Keep informed” and “keep satisfied” belong to different combinations. A job title alone is not a substitute for assessing and plotting the stakeholder’s power and interest in this work.''','Keep message schedules for engagement planning; teach the axes and four approaches.','HIGH','diagram_animation','Plotting examples across two axes makes the four engagement approaches and their contrasts clear.')
add(20,'''Imagine the task is reviewing delivery-instruction requirements. A hypothetical RACI row assigns the BA as Responsible for doing the review preparation, the operations manager as Accountable for the task’s completion, dispatch and sales as Consulted, and the sponsor as Informed.

Read each letter as a different relationship to this task. Responsible identifies the person or people doing the work. Accountable identifies the one person ultimately answerable for completion. Consulted participants provide input through two-way communication; Informed participants receive updates through one-way communication. The row therefore does not simply mark who attends a meeting.

Build the chart by listing tasks against stakeholders and assigning appropriate R, A, C, or I entries. For a second example task, “Prepare an instruction-access demonstration,” the role assignments might differ. Do not carry the first row’s assignments over merely because the same people are involved. Check that each task has one accountable owner.

In the first row, asking dispatch for input fits C; sending the sponsor a progress update fits I. If two people are marked A for the same task, the row does not meet the one-accountable-owner check. Resolve the ownership assignment rather than leaving two competing A entries. The useful result is task-specific clarity about work, ownership, consultation, and updates, not a general ranking of stakeholder seniority.''','''Responsible and Accountable are not interchangeable. Several people can do the work, while the task has one ultimately answerable owner. Consulted also differs from Informed: input requires two-way communication; receiving an update is one-way.''','Keep assignments hypothetical; do not prescribe the BA as accountable for business approval.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Building and checking task rows makes R/A ownership and C/I communication differences visible.')
add(21,'''Suppose stakeholder analysis shows an operations manager with high power and interest and dispatch staff with high interest but lower power. Engagement planning turns those assessments into specific ongoing communication actions rather than leaving them as labels on a grid.

For this hypothetical plan, the manager’s communication goal is to resolve scope decisions. The channel is a short decision meeting, the frequency is weekly during requirements review, and the owner is the BA preparing the materials. Dispatch staff’s goal is to understand current requirements and supply feedback; the channel is a shared update and discussion, with a stated frequency and owner. These channels and timings are examples chosen for this situation, not a mandatory schedule.

For every stakeholder group, make the goal, channel, frequency, and owner explicit. Then check whether the planned intensity fits the group’s power and interest. An assessment has practical value only when it informs actual engagement choices.

Review effectiveness too. If a group remains unclear about what is being agreed, consider whether the approach is meeting its communication goal. The plan is not merely proof that messages were sent. It should connect who is involved, why communication is needed, how it will occur, and who owns it. The example stays within communication planning; it does not transfer business decision authority to the person sending the update.''','''A list of channels is not yet an engagement plan. Each group needs a communication goal, frequency, and owner as well. Sending an update also does not by itself establish that the approach is effective; review it against the intended engagement goal.''','Use example frequencies only, not universal stakeholder rules.')
add(22,'''Imagine a requirements meeting where one sales representative answers every question before dispatch staff can speak. The facilitator’s task is to support the group’s collaborative process, not to let the loudest contribution become the whole record.

Plan an agenda with activities and allotted time that support the meeting’s goal. For this example, the goal is to clarify delivery-instruction needs, so allocate time for individual input, shared discussion, and consolidating the needs. During discussion, redirect a dominating contribution toward the shared goal or invite others to contribute: “We have the sales perspective; what does dispatch need at that point?” This keeps guidance connected to the work rather than making it a contest between participants.

If verbal discussion still prevents equitable participation, use silent individual contributions. Participants could each write the instruction information they need before the group discusses it. That is an example of the supported technique, not a claim that silent writing is required in every meeting.

The facilitator plans, guides, and manages the process so the group can work collaboratively. The useful result is not merely finishing every agenda item on time: activities and time need to support the goals, and discussion needs room for balanced contributions. Keep the facilitation intervention focused on participation and shared purpose rather than deciding whose requirement is correct on the group’s behalf.''','''Facilitation is not the same as speaking most or choosing the group’s solution. Redirecting dominance and inviting input support the collaborative process. Silent contributions are an option when verbal discussion prevents equitable participation, not a compulsory ritual for every workshop.''','Do not introduce consensus or voting as substitutes for balanced participation.','MEDIUM','talking_head_with_visual_support','A facilitated discussion demonstration can show redirection, invitations, and a switch to silent contributions.')
add(23,'''Suppose sales wants very few fields on a delivery-instruction form, while dispatch wants enough information to avoid clarification calls. Their goals and measures of success differ: one emphasizes easy entry, the other complete dispatch information. Naming that difference is more useful than presenting one side’s preferred design as an isolated solution.

Bring stakeholders into discussion of the goals and evidence. For this hypothetical case, examine examples of instruction entry and clarification work together. Ask what each group is trying to achieve and which evidence relates to that goal. The purpose is shared understanding of the competing needs, not hiding the disagreement behind a vague claim that everyone wants a better process.

Forced ranking or dot voting can help the group move toward consensus. For example, participants could rank the information needs being discussed or place dots beside the needs they consider most important. The technique supports movement toward agreement; the brief does not say that the highest vote automatically overrides every constraint or proves the solution is correct.

The useful BA contribution is a discussion in which stakeholders can see one another’s goals and evidence before moving toward a shared position. Keep any remaining disagreement visible rather than announcing consensus merely because a voting activity took place. The example fields and goals are hypothetical prompts for that discussion.''','''Consensus building is not presenting a ready-made solution and asking for compliance. Voting or ranking can help, but the supported claim is that these techniques can move a group toward consensus—not that a vote guarantees complete agreement or correctness.''','Avoid unsupported negotiation frameworks or claims that majority vote is binding.','MEDIUM','talking_head_with_visual_support','A group discussion example can show how goals and evidence are surfaced before a ranking or voting activity.')
add(24,'''Imagine representing a simple dispatch process: an order arrives, staff prepare it, a decision determines whether clarification is needed, and the path ends after dispatch preparation. BPMN notation gives each kind of element a distinct visual role rather than drawing every step as the same box.

Draw the arrival as a thin single-circle start event and “Prepare order” as a rounded-rectangle task. Draw the branching decision as a diamond gateway. Connect execution order within the process using solid lines with solid arrowheads: these are sequence flows. If something occurs between start and end, select a thin double-circle intermediate event; mark the end of a process path with a thick single-circle end event. The example does not require every event type on every path.

Suppose dispatch sends a request to a separate participant, such as an external delivery service. Represent that communication with a dashed message flow having an open circle and open arrowhead, not with the solid sequence flow used for execution order. The diagram now distinguishes something happening, an atomic activity, routing, and communication between participants.

When reading it back, name both the element and what it represents in this process. “Diamond” describes the shape; “gateway controlling branching and convergence” explains its function. Keep the notation tied to the work rather than memorizing disconnected shapes.''','''A sequence flow and a message flow do different jobs. Solid sequence arrows indicate execution order within a process; dashed message flows show communication between separate participants. Start, intermediate, and end events are also distinguished by their circle outlines, not all drawn identically.''','Teach only supported core shapes and flows; avoid gateway subtypes, pools, lanes, and advanced markers.','HIGH','whiteboard_drawing','Drawing the process reveals shape distinctions, event outlines, execution order, and separate-participant communication.')
add(25,'''Suppose dispatch work currently begins when an order arrives and ends when preparation is complete. You want a record of that existing behavior, not a cleaner version of what you hope staff will do.

Identify the start and end, then collect information about the actual steps and decisions. In this hypothetical process, staff read the order, check instructions, decide whether clarification is needed, request clarification if necessary, and prepare dispatch. Determine the sequence and routing from that information. Represent the activities with BPMN tasks, use appropriate events and gateways, and connect execution order with sequence flows.

If clarification is inconvenient, keep it in the as-is map when it is part of the current process. Removing it because it looks inefficient would change the map from a current-state record to a proposal. Review the map with people who perform the process. They may explain that clarification happens before another step, or that the map omitted an activity. Refine the map to reflect their review.

The practical output is a current-process model with a supported sequence and routing. Accuracy is the immediate aim here. A later improvement discussion can use that baseline, but the as-is map should not silently merge observed behavior with desired changes.''','''As-is means current, not ideal. A troublesome step still belongs if it is part of current work. Review with the people performing the process refines accuracy; it is not permission to replace current behavior with a proposed redesign.''','Keep improvements as a later comparison, not embedded in the as-is map.','HIGH','whiteboard_drawing','Building and reviewing a current-state BPMN map makes observed sequencing and routing concrete.')
add(26,'''Imagine the current dispatch map includes a separate instruction-sheet lookup and a clarification request. The team proposes a future process in which current instructions are available at the order step. A to-be map shows that intended future behavior, not a claim that the change has already happened.

Use the current-state map to identify the proposed improvements. In this hypothetical redesign, replace the separate lookup with “Read current instructions on order,” and revise the routing for clarification according to the proposed behavior. Draw the changed activities using BPMN tasks, appropriate events and gateways, and sequence flows. Keep the revised map understandable as a complete proposed process rather than a few isolated change labels.

Read the old and revised sequences separately. The as-is sequence records the current lookup; the to-be sequence reflects the proposed instruction-access change. Their different meanings matter even when both use the same BPMN shapes.

The useful output is a future-state map that explicitly represents intended activities and routing after improvement. The example is a proposal, not evidence of achieved efficiency. A revised diagram can communicate how the process is meant to work while leaving implementation and actual results to their own work.''','''To-be does not mean already operating. Do not describe a proposed route as observed behavior or assume that redrawing a process proves an improvement has been achieved. The model represents the intended future state.''','Use the current map as input; avoid claiming measured benefits from a proposal.','HIGH','whiteboard_drawing','A before-and-proposed-after drawing shows how activities and routing change between maps.')
add(27,'''Suppose the dispatch map shows orders accumulating before an approval step. A delay label alone does not establish the full bottleneck explanation. In this hypothetical case, the step can handle ten orders per hour while fifteen arrive, and the waiting work grows. Annotate the map with that capacity and accumulating-work evidence: the step cannot keep pace and limits the workflow’s throughput.

Inspect other mapped steps and handoffs for obstacles, redundancy, or missing work. Perhaps the same instructions are copied at two handoffs. List the specific steps where the map shows those issues and review the findings with process stakeholders. Do not group every issue under “bottleneck” merely because it is inconvenient.

Compare current-state and future-state maps too. If the proposal removes one copying step, identify that concrete difference and discuss its potential efficiency gain. A map comparison identifies a proposed difference; it is not itself a measured result.

The output should connect each finding to a location in the process and supporting evidence. For the approval bottleneck, the key evidence is capacity that cannot keep pace, accumulated work, and the resulting throughput limitation. For redundant copying, the finding is repeated effort. These distinctions make the analysis more informative than marking every slow-looking box red.''','''Not every delay is a bottleneck. The supported bottleneck definition requires a capacity-limited step that cannot keep pace, causing accumulation and limiting throughput. Also label future-state efficiency gains as potential until supported by actual results.''','Keep capacity examples hypothetical; distinguish bottlenecks from redundancy and missing work.','HIGH','diagram_animation','Showing work accumulating at a capacity-limited step and comparing mapped handoffs clarifies bottleneck evidence.')
add(28,'''Imagine the process map shows dispatch instructions copied twice before preparation. The team investigates that repeated effort as a possible non-value-adding step. Lean improvement aims to eliminate waste and improve the value delivered by the process; the example is about examining a specific source of waste, not removing work simply because it takes time.

Inspect the copying steps and propose a change that removes or reduces the identified waste. In this hypothetical situation, the team proposes using the current instruction record instead of making a second copy. Agree the improvement action with the team, implement it, and review its effects.

The review might examine whether the repeated copying was reduced and whether the process now delivers the intended value more effectively. Those are questions for the review, not results you can infer from the proposal alone. If the effects show further issues, continue improving the process rather than treating the first change as the final answer.

The BA’s analysis connects an observed process feature, identified waste, an agreed action, and a review of effects. The technique remains grounded in the work: “remove a box from the map” is less informative than explaining which non-value-adding activity is being reduced and then checking the change.''','''Improvement is not synonymous with deleting as many steps as possible. Inspect for identified non-value-adding work, agree an action, and review its effects after implementation. A proposed simplification is not yet evidence that value or efficiency improved.''','Do not add unsupported Lean waste categories or mandatory improvement cycles.','MEDIUM','whiteboard_drawing','A process example can show identified repeated effort, the proposed change, and what would be reviewed afterward.')
add(29,'''Suppose stakeholders say amended delivery instructions require supervisor approval. That approval governs what the deliverable must allow; it should not remain an informal comment outside the requirements record.

In this hypothetical entry, document “An amended instruction requires supervisor approval before dispatch preparation continues.” Record the operating constraint explicitly alongside the required behavior. If the team assumes that a supervisor is available during dispatch hours, capture that assumption too rather than leaving it hidden in the sentence.

The distinction is practical: a requested function might be displaying or amending instructions, while the governing approval or constraint limits how the work may proceed. This brief supports capturing those assumptions, constraints, and approvals; it does not provide a complete taxonomy of business rules.

Read the requirement record back to stakeholders with the approval made visible. A description that says only “Users can amend instructions” omits the stated governing condition in this example. The useful output is explicit required approval and operating-constraint information linked to what the deliverable must do. The supervisor rule is invented for the exercise, not a general policy for order services.''','''An approval rule should not be left as background prose while the function is documented without it. Capture governing assumptions and constraints explicitly. Do not invent company policies or a complete business-rule classification beyond what the requirements evidence supports.''','Coverage is narrow: focus on explicit approvals, assumptions, and constraints; do not expand to an unsupported rule taxonomy.')
add(30,'''Imagine a hypothetical policy with two conditions: whether an order is urgent and whether approval is present. The written rule says an urgent approved order may proceed; an urgent unapproved order requests approval; a nonurgent order may proceed regardless of approval.

Construct a limited-entry decision table with condition rows “Urgent?” and “Approved?” and action rows “Proceed” and “Request approval.” Give each rule a column. Rule 1 has T/T and X for Proceed. Rule 2 has T/F and X for Request approval. Rule 3 has F/– and X for Proceed. Leave the other action cells blank. Here – means the approval condition is irrelevant to the nonurgent outcome, so Rule 3 covers F/T and F/F.

Check completeness by listing the feasible combinations T/T, T/F, F/T, and F/F. All four have an outcome in this example. If Rule 2 were missing, T/F would be a gap. Infeasible combinations could be removed in a different policy, but none are declared infeasible here.

Check consistency against the written rules. If another rule assigns Request approval instead of Proceed to T/T, the same feasible combination has incompatible outcomes. Record that contradiction for resolution rather than choosing one arbitrarily. The table’s job is to expose condition-to-action logic, including gaps and contradictions, not conceal uncertainty behind a neat grid.''','''A dash is not false: it means the condition is irrelevant to the outcome. Completeness and consistency are separate checks. A table can cover every feasible combination and still contradict the written rules by assigning incompatible outcomes to the same combination.''','Use conditions as rows and rules as columns; avoid reversing the brief convention.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Filling rule columns and checking every feasible combination makes dash expansion, gaps, and contradictions visible.')
add(31,'''Suppose a hypothetical dispatch decision first asks whether an order is urgent. If yes, it next asks whether approval is present. If no, it proceeds through the nonurgent outcome. A decision tree makes that sequence of choices and possible outcomes visible with branches.

Start with the initial decision “Urgent?” Add one branch for yes and another for no. On the yes branch, add the subsequent decision “Approval present?” and branches for its possible answers. The approved path ends at “Proceed”; the unapproved path ends at “Request approval.” In this exercise, the no-urgency branch ends at “Proceed.” These are invented policy outcomes, not general rules for dispatch.

Read a path from the initial decision through the relevant answers to its outcome. An urgent order without approval follows yes at the first decision and no at the second, reaching Request approval. This explanation ties branch labels to decisions instead of using unlabeled lines.

The useful output is sequential branching logic. Do not add a subsequent decision to every branch merely to make the drawing symmetrical; continue where the decision being represented requires it. The tree communicates choices and outcomes, not the statistical prediction methods that may use a similarly named structure elsewhere.''','''A branch represents a possible answer or choice leading onward; it is not just decoration around a list of outcomes. Keep the initial and subsequent decisions visible so the reader can follow the sequence. This is business decision logic, not a machine-learning lesson.''','Avoid decision-table completeness rules being silently recast as tree-specific facts.','HIGH','whiteboard_drawing','Drawing and tracing one path makes ordered decisions, answer branches, and final outcomes easy to follow.')
add(32,'''Imagine a stakeholder saying, “We need to describe customers and their orders, including the customer name and each order’s requested delivery date.” Start by identifying the business things being described: Customer and Order. Then separate their descriptive properties.

For this hypothetical domain, list Customer attributes such as customer name and contact number, and Order attributes such as requested delivery date and instruction text. The attribute belongs to the thing it describes. A requested delivery date describes an order in this example, while customer name describes a customer.

Read another sentence: “Dispatch staff need the instruction text for an order.” It adds a descriptive need for Order rather than automatically introducing a new entity called InstructionText. The question is which business thing needs describing and which properties are needed for that description.

The output can remain a plain list of entities with attributes. This step does not require keys, relationship symbols, or a database implementation. Those details come later. Focus first on distinguishing a business thing from a descriptive property of that thing, using the narrative as evidence rather than adding every imaginable attribute. The resulting list should answer what the business needs to describe in this example.''','''An attribute is not automatically a separate entity because its name appears in a sentence. Ask whether it describes a business thing already identified. Conversely, do not merge unrelated business things into one vague entity just because they appear in the same narrative.''','Keep keys and relationships for subsequent concepts; use ordinary business descriptions first.')
add(33,'''Suppose a Customer table has rows for customers 101, 102, and 103. CustomerID is a candidate for the primary key if each row’s value is unique and not null. CustomerName is less suitable in an example where two different customers share the name “North Stores”: the repeated name would not uniquely identify each row.

A table has at most one primary key, but that key can span multiple columns. Imagine a separate table of branch-specific order numbers in which order number 7 occurs at both North and South branches. The pair BranchCode and OrderNumber can form a composite key if every pair is unique and neither part is null. One key composed of two attributes is not two primary keys.

Check the sample rows deliberately. Duplicate CustomerID values violate the uniqueness requirement. A missing CustomerID violates the not-null requirement. For a composite key, check the combination for uniqueness and every component for null values. A row with a known branch but a null order number does not meet entity integrity.

The practical result is identifying the column or column group that can uniquely identify a row while meeting the stated integrity conditions. A convenient-looking label is not enough: the actual values must satisfy uniqueness and non-null requirements.''','''Unique is not the whole primary-key rule: values must also be not null, and no part of a composite primary key may be null. A composite key is one primary key spanning columns, not permission to assign several primary keys to a table.''','Distinguish candidate examples from declared keys; avoid CREATE TABLE syntax.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Checking rows for duplicate and null single-column or composite identifiers makes the integrity requirements visible.')
add(34,'''Imagine Customer has primary-key values 101 and 102. Order contains CustomerID to refer to a customer. An order with CustomerID 101 has a matching referenced row; a non-null CustomerID 999 does not in this hypothetical data.

The foreign-key constraint requires non-null referencing values to match a value in the referenced table. A row with 999 would be an orphan record if that value is absent from Customer. Referential integrity prevents that unmatched reference rather than making up a new customer to satisfy it.

A null foreign-key value also satisfies referential integrity under the rule in this brief. That is different from primary-key rules, where null is not allowed. For the exercise, distinguish three cases: matching 101, unmatched non-null 999, and null. Do not describe all three as simply “valid customer values”: null represents no known referenced value here, while 101 matches an existing customer.

A foreign key can also use a group of columns when the reference requires it. The learner’s immediate task is to check whether the referencing value or values match the required referenced row, while preserving the stated null exception. Keep the example about references and integrity rather than adding deletion or update behavior that has not been established.''','''The foreign-key null rule differs from the primary-key rule. In this brief, null satisfies referential integrity; a non-null value must match. Do not treat an unmatched non-null value as an acceptable unknown.''','Do not invent cascade behavior or NULL prohibitions beyond the brief.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Tracing matching, null, and orphan references across two small tables clarifies referential integrity.')
add(35,'''Suppose Customer has one row for customer 101 and one for customer 102. Order has three rows referencing 101 and no rows referencing 102. One customer can therefore relate to many orders, while the example allows a customer to have zero orders.

Identify Order as the many side. Its CustID foreign key stores the reference to a valid Customer CustID. The same customer identifier can appear on multiple order rows because those rows are different orders belonging to the same customer. The Customer side has the customer row being referenced.

Read the allowed counts in both directions. Customer 101 has several orders; customer 102 has none and still exists as a customer. Each order relates to a maximum of one customer in the stated example. Do not reverse the relationship merely because the customer name is shown on an order report.

The useful modeling decision is not just naming “one-to-many.” It is identifying which thing can have many related instances and placing the reference on that many side. The zero-order customer also shows why “many” should not be interpreted as requiring at least one order in this example. These counts are the business rules of the Customer/Order example, not universal counts for every relationship.''','''One-to-many does not mean every one-side row already has several related rows. The stated customer example permits zero orders. Nor should the foreign key be placed on Customer just because that is the one side: CustID belongs in Order for this relationship.''','Preserve the zero-to-many customer example and maximum-one wording for an order.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Linking one customer to several orders and another to none makes direction, optionality, and foreign-key placement visible.')
add(36,'''Imagine orders can include several products, and a product can appear in several orders. That is a many-to-many relationship. In the relational model described here, resolve it using a junction table rather than attempting to implement the many-to-many relationship directly.

For this hypothetical model, create OrderProduct between Order and Product. It contains OrderID and ProductID, brought in from the original primary keys and used as foreign keys. It may also contain its own attribute, such as quantity. The two original tables now each have a one-to-many relationship with OrderProduct.

Example junction rows are (Order 10, Product A, quantity 2), (Order 10, Product B, quantity 1), and (Order 11, Product A, quantity 3). Order 10 links to two products, and Product A links to two orders. The pair of original identifiers forms the junction primary key in this example. The brief also allows those identifiers together with another attribute to form the key where needed.

The important change is the representation of each association as a junction row. Quantity describes that association here, not every occurrence of Product A in every order. Keep both original references visible so the model does not lose one side of the relationship when resolving it.''','''A junction table is not simply a copy of one original table. It contains at least both original primary keys as foreign keys, and its key uses their combination or that combination with another attribute. Resolving M:N produces two 1:M relationships.''','Do not introduce alternative surrogate-key designs beyond this brief.','HIGH','diagram_animation','Transforming a many-to-many association into junction rows and two one-to-many links makes the resolution understandable.')
add(37,'''Suppose business rules say a customer may have zero or many orders, while each order must relate to exactly one customer. Cardinality expresses permitted counts, not merely the number of rows currently in a sample.

Check each direction: one customer can match multiple orders, and one order matches one customer. In crow’s-foot notation, place circle plus crow’s-foot at the Order end for zero-or-many orders per customer. Place two bars at the Customer end for mandatory exactly-one customer per order. At each end, put the minimum symbol inward and the maximum symbol closest to the entity box.

The symbols carry separate meanings: circle means zero, bar means one, and crow’s-foot means many. Circle plus bar would mean optional zero-or-one; bar plus crow’s-foot would mean mandatory one-or-many. Use the pair appropriate to the actual business rule instead of choosing whichever shape fits the available space.

The data perspective also helps: one-to-many has unique values on one side and potentially repeated matching values on the other; many-to-many permits repeated matching values on both sides. Determine the relationship by checking whether each side permits one or multiple matching instances. The example’s mandatory order-to-customer rule is a hypothetical business rule for this diagram, not inferred solely from observing one current order.''','''A relationship end describes how many instances of that end’s entity can relate to an instance of the opposite entity. Reading the crow’s-foot as the count of customers instead of orders reverses the example. A current sample also does not by itself establish all permitted counts.''','Keep minimum/maximum symbol ordering explicit and do not confuse cardinality with observed row counts.','HIGH','whiteboard_drawing','Annotating both relationship ends makes crow’s-foot direction, minimum, and maximum counts visible.')
add(38,'''Imagine a business model containing Customer and Order. Draw a named box for each entity and list relevant attributes. For this hypothetical diagram, Customer contains CustomerID and Name; Order contains OrderID, CustomerID, and RequestedDate. Clearly identify CustomerID as Customer’s primary key, OrderID as Order’s primary key, and Order.CustomerID as a foreign key.

Connect the foreign-key attribute to its referenced key. Mark permitted multiplicities on the relationship: suppose every order belongs to exactly one customer and each customer may have zero or many orders. Use 1 at the Customer end and 0..* at the Order end for those rules. A 0..1 label would instead allow zero or one; 1..* would require one or many.

Read the diagram back in business language. It says which things are described, what properties they have, how their instances relate, and how many related instances are permitted. The links should correspond to the identified keys rather than being arbitrary lines between boxes.

The result is an entity-relationship diagram, not just a list of tables. The particular attributes and mandatory customer rule are invented for this exercise. In actual modeling, use the established domain information and permitted counts rather than borrowing this example’s rules automatically.''','''Two boxes joined by an unlabeled line do not communicate the full permitted multiplicities. Likewise, identifying keys without connecting a foreign key to its referenced key leaves the intended relationship unclear. Keep both structural links and allowed counts visible.''','Avoid tool-specific ERD commands or additional notation not in the facts.','HIGH','whiteboard_drawing','Drawing boxes, identifying keys, and labeling relationship ends shows how the diagram communicates a model.')
add(39,'''Suppose one model says the business describes customers and their orders, with customer characteristics and an order relationship. It gives a high-level view of the domain rather than a technical database implementation. Classify that as conceptual based on its business focus and abstraction.

Another model details the attributes, including data types and lengths, identifiers, and specified relationships. Classify that as logical when it remains implementation-independent. For example, it might identify CustomerID as an entity identifier and give Name a text type and stated length. In Oracle Data Modeler, a primary unique identifier can be composed of attributes, and foreign unique identifiers refer to identifiers of another entity.

The distinction rests on level of detail, not on whether the model contains business words. Both can describe the same domain. A conceptual overview emphasizes important entity classes, characteristics, constraints, and relationships; a logical model supplies a less abstract structure without being a technical database implementation.''','''Logical does not mean a specific implemented database. Both classifications here are distinct from technical implementation. Use the detail and implementation independence as evidence, not the drawing’s title alone.''','Do not expand to physical modeling or vendor implementation rules.')
add(40,'''Suppose a stakeholder asks, “Which customer placed each order, and which requested delivery dates belong to those orders?” Translate that information need into the entities and attributes that can supply it.

In this hypothetical domain, Customer.Name supplies the customer description, Order.RequestedDate supplies the requested date, and the established customer-order relationship connects the two. If the stakeholder wants to filter by branch, look for the entity attribute that actually records branch. If none has been identified, do not pretend the information is available just because the report needs it.

Record a mapping such as “customer name → Customer.Name” and “requested delivery date → Order.RequestedDate.” This makes the relationship between the reporting or filtering request and modeled information explicit. The mapping is conceptual work here; it does not require writing SQL or opening a dashboard tool.

The practical output is a supported account of which attributes provide the requested business information. A stakeholder phrase and an attribute name need not be identical, so explain the match in plain language. Conversely, matching words alone is not enough if the attribute describes something else. Keep the business question visible while checking the model’s entities and descriptive attributes.''','''Wanting a report does not establish that all needed attributes exist. Match each reporting or filtering need to attributes that can supply it; do not silently invent missing information or introduce query syntax before it is taught.''','Stay at needs-to-attributes mapping, not SQL or BI authoring.')
add(41,'''Imagine the business keeps Customer data and Order data. In the relational model, each named table holds rows of related values. A Customer row could contain one customer’s identifier, name, and contact information; its columns are the fields or attributes describing that kind of thing.

An Order table has its own distinct name and holds order data. Together, multiple tables compose the database. Keep each table about one kind of thing rather than mixing unrelated customer and vehicle descriptions in an arbitrary row.

The relation has no duplicate rows, and the sequence of rows and columns is insignificant to its relational meaning. If two customer rows are displayed in a different order, the display position is not itself a customer attribute. Do not use “the third row” as the business identity of a customer.

The useful mental model is a named table with meaningful fields and related values in each record, within a database of multiple tables. The sample values are hypothetical and the treatment is the relational model stated in the brief. It is not yet a lesson in query output ordering or every behavior a database product may permit. First be able to identify a table, row, and column in the business data.''','''A row is a group of related data values, not an arbitrary bundle of unrelated things. Table position is also not relational meaning: row and column sequence is insignificant. Keep the relational model distinct from assumptions about how a tool happens to display data.''','Do not introduce SQL commands or claim every SQL product forbids duplicate stored rows.','LOW','data_table_demo','A small labeled table is sufficient to distinguish records and fields without a motion demonstration.')
add(42,'''Suppose Orders has columns OrderID, CustomerID, and Amount, and a stakeholder needs only identifiers and amounts. SELECT lets you name the desired output columns without asking for every field in the table.

For a hypothetical table with rows (10, 101, 40) and (11, 102, 60), write:
SELECT OrderID, Amount FROM Orders;

The output contains OrderID and Amount: the value pairs are (10, 40) and (11, 60). CustomerID was not named in the select list, so it is not one of the output columns. These pairs describe the result contents, not a promised row order.

If you instead write:
SELECT * FROM Orders;
then the result contains all three columns from this example table. The star means all columns, while the named-column form states which columns you want.

Read the query as a request: retrieve OrderID and Amount from Orders. The SELECT list tells you what data appears in the output; FROM tells you the table being queried. The immediate BA use is retrieving the fields needed for a business question. This example does not yet filter, join, total, or sort the data: keep the distinction between choosing output columns and those later operations clear.''','''SELECT * means all columns, not a special calculation or a chosen business subset. Naming two columns chooses those output fields. It does not by itself select particular rows or establish an output order.''','Teach only SELECT lists and FROM; no filtering, aggregation, aliases, or CREATE TABLE.','LOW','annotated_code','The two simple retrieval forms can be compared in a short annotated text example.')
add(43,'''Imagine Customers contains (101, North Stores) and (102, South Stores), while Orders contains order 10 for customer 101 and order 11 for customer 999. A report needs an order identifier paired with its matching customer name.

Write:
SELECT Orders.OrderID, Customers.Name FROM Orders INNER JOIN Customers ON Orders.CustomerID = Customers.CustomerID;

The join condition compares the two customer identifiers. Order 10 matches customer 101, producing (10, North Stores). Order 11 has no matching customer row in this deliberately unmatched reporting example, so it is dropped. Customer 102 has no matching order and is also dropped. The unmatched value illustrates join behavior; it is not a valid foreign-key example under an enforced matching constraint.

An INNER JOIN produces a joined row for every satisfying pair, not simply one result for each input table. If multiple rows on the other side satisfy the condition, each matching pair contributes a joined row. The condition can be expressed with ON, or USING when both tables use the same column name. JOIN by itself, without a different join kind, means INNER JOIN.

The practical reporting question is therefore which matches the condition creates and which rows lack matches. Do that reasoning before assuming the result includes every customer or every order.''','''An inner join does not preserve unmatched rows. Also, “one match per row” is not its general rule: each satisfying pair produces a joined row. In this sample there is one matching pair, but that is a property of the data, not a universal join limit.''','Avoid outer joins and NULL behavior until their entries; unmatched sample intentionally has no enforced FK.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Tracing pairs across two tables makes matching combinations and dropped unmatched rows visible.')
add(44,'''Suppose Orders contains three hypothetical rows: North with Amount 40, North with Amount 60, and South with Amount 30. A stakeholder asks for total amount per branch, not the original order-level rows.

Write:
SELECT Branch, SUM(Amount) FROM Orders GROUP BY Branch;

GROUP BY gathers rows with the same Branch value into one result group. SUM computes the total within each group. North contributes 40 + 60 = 100, and South contributes 30. The result contents are therefore (North, 100) and (South, 30), with no output order promised by this query.

In the ordinary grouping pattern, each selected column outside an aggregate function belongs in GROUP BY. Branch is the unaggregated selected column here, while Amount is inside SUM. If the required question changes to totals per branch and service category, the grouping needs to reflect those selected grouping columns rather than pretending one branch total also identifies a single category.

The practical change is the level of the result: several source rows can contribute to one grouped total. Explain that to a stakeholder who expects to see individual orders. The query is not deleting those source details; its requested output is a group-level summary.''','''Selecting a group label and a total does not make every other source field meaningful at the group level. Follow the ordinary pattern of listing selected nonaggregate columns in GROUP BY. Also distinguish a per-group total from one original row’s amount.''','Stay with SUM and ordinary grouping; do not introduce other aggregates or vendor exceptions.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Gathering repeated branch values into groups and adding amounts makes the change in result level visible.')
add(45,'''Imagine Orders has North amounts 40, 60, and -10, and South amount 30. The business question is “Which branches have more than 90 in positive order amounts?” It contains a row-level condition and a group-level condition.

Write:
SELECT Branch, SUM(Amount) FROM Orders WHERE Amount > 0 GROUP BY Branch HAVING SUM(Amount) > 90;

WHERE retains input rows with positive Amount before grouping. North’s -10 row is excluded, leaving 40 and 60; South’s 30 remains. GROUP BY then creates branch groups and SUM gives North 100 and South 30. HAVING retains groups whose total exceeds 90, leaving North with 100.

The order of those roles matters. The positive-amount condition applies to each input row, while the more-than-90 condition applies to the calculated group total. Describing both as simply “filters” hides that distinction.

The comparison operators express the tests: = means equal, <> not equal, and < or > less or greater than. In this example, > 90 excludes a total equal to 90. Keep the query connected to the business question so the learner can tell which condition belongs before grouping and which belongs afterward.''','''WHERE does not filter already-calculated groups in this pattern, and HAVING is not a substitute for the positive-row condition. Filtering inputs can change totals; filtering groups decides which calculated totals remain. An amount threshold and a total threshold are different requirements.''','Keep the supported WHERE → grouping → HAVING ordering explicit.','HIGH','data_table_demo','A staged row-filter, group-total, and group-filter demonstration reveals how each condition changes the result.')
add(46,'''Suppose Orders has Amount values 40, NULL, and 0. The missing value is unknown; it is not the same as a known zero. SQL types also distinguish categories such as integer identifiers, exact monetary values, text, dates, and Boolean values. Choose types whose categories and ranges fit what the business stores.

To retrieve the missing-amount rows, use:
SELECT OrderID, Amount FROM Orders WHERE Amount IS NULL;
For known amounts, use IS NOT NULL instead. Do not replace that test with = NULL. Ordinary comparisons involving NULL yield unknown, and WHERE keeps only rows whose condition is true.

For a display that explicitly substitutes zero when Amount is missing, write:
SELECT OrderID, COALESCE(Amount, 0) FROM Orders;
With sample rows (10, 40), (11, NULL), and (12, 0), the displayed pairs are (10, 40), (11, 0), and (12, 0). COALESCE returns the first non-NULL argument. The display choice does not prove the original unknown amount was truly zero.

Likewise, WHERE Amount > 0 keeps the 40 row, not the NULL row or the zero row. The NULL comparison is unknown rather than false or true, and unknown does not pass WHERE. Preserving that distinction matters when interpreting what a reporting filter excluded.''','''NULL is an unknown value, not automatically zero or empty text. A substitution in SELECT changes the returned display value, not the business meaning of the missing source value. Use IS NULL or IS NOT NULL for missing-value tests rather than ordinary equality.''','Avoid complete type catalogues or unintroduced casts; retain unknown-versus-zero distinction.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Comparing NULL, zero, tests, and substituted outputs makes missing-value behavior concrete.')
add(47,'''Suppose Customers contains (101, North Stores) and (102, South Stores). Orders contains (10, 101) and (11, 101). The reporting question is “List all customers, with their orders where any match.” Put Customers on the left so that unmatched customer rows are preserved.

Write:
SELECT a.CustomerID, b.OrderID FROM Customers AS a LEFT OUTER JOIN Orders AS b ON a.CustomerID = b.CustomerID;

Customer 101 has two matches, producing (101, 10) and (101, 11). Customer 102 has no matching order, so it still appears, with NULL for the right-table OrderID: (102, NULL). Here a and b are the table aliases used in the supplied join pattern.

The result combines matching rows and a preserved unmatched left row. It is not limited to one output row per customer: multiple matching orders produce multiple combinations. NULL in the unmatched row represents the missing right-side fields, not an order identifier equal to zero.

The important decision is which table must remain represented when there is no match. In this example that is Customers. Reversing the table placement without changing the intended join would change which side is guaranteed preservation, so read the FROM and LEFT OUTER JOIN sides together with the business question.''','''“Left” refers to table placement in the query, not business importance. A left outer join preserves unmatched left rows and supplies NULL right fields for them. It still returns matching combinations rather than collapsing all matches into one row.''','Keep table-preservation logic central; do not introduce reporting totals or distinct-row syntax.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Showing a customer with two orders and one with none demonstrates matching combinations and left preservation.')
add(48,'''Use a hypothetical report where Orders contains (10, 101) and (11, 101), and Customers contains (101, North Stores) and (102, South Stores). You want all customers represented even if a customer has no orders. With Customers placed on the right, a right outer join preserves that side.

Write:
SELECT a.OrderID, b.CustomerID FROM Orders AS a RIGHT OUTER JOIN Customers AS b ON a.CustomerID = b.CustomerID;

The matching results include (10, 101) and (11, 101). Customer 102 has no matching order, so the result also includes (NULL, 102). The missing values are from the left table, Orders, because this is an unmatched right-table row.

Compare table position with result interpretation. It is Customers that must remain represented for this business question, and Customers is on the right of RIGHT OUTER JOIN. The join returns matching combinations as well as preserved unmatched right rows. It does not imply that every right row has a real order.

The practical skill is choosing and reading preservation correctly. A report reader seeing NULL for OrderID should understand that the preserved customer lacked a matching left-table row under this condition. Do not turn that missing match into a fabricated order number.''','''Right outer join preservation follows the right-hand table in the query. For an unmatched right row, left-table fields are NULL. That is the opposite missing-side placement from an unmatched left row in a left outer join.''','Do not describe right preservation as preserving the first FROM table.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Tracing the preserved right side makes unmatched right rows and NULL left fields unmistakable.')
add(49,'''Imagine comparing two independently supplied extracts, rather than tables with an enforced foreign-key relationship. Orders contains (10, 101) and (11, 999); Customers contains (101, North Stores) and (102, South Stores). You want matching combinations plus unmatched rows from either extract.

Write:
SELECT a.OrderID, b.CustomerID FROM Orders AS a FULL OUTER JOIN Customers AS b ON a.CustomerID = b.CustomerID;

The matching pair produces (10, 101). Order 11 has no customer match, so it produces (11, NULL). Customer 102 has no order match, so it produces (NULL, 102). These are result contents, not a guaranteed sequence.

For each unmatched row, fields on the missing side are filled with NULL. The join therefore preserves unmatched rows from both tables instead of choosing only the left or the right side. The sample’s unmatched identifier is deliberate reporting data; it would not satisfy a matching foreign-key constraint.

The business interpretation is that both extracts remain represented under the chosen condition, including rows needing further investigation. A full outer join does not itself repair unmatched references, infer missing customer details, or declare every preserved row valid. Its supported role here is returning matching combinations and preserving unmatched rows from either side.''','''Preserving unmatched rows is not the same as resolving their missing match. Full outer join supplies NULL for fields from the absent side; it does not invent that side’s data. Matching combinations still appear alongside the unmatched rows.''','Use independent extracts for deliberate unmatched identifiers; do not imply valid FK orphans.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Showing one match and unmatched rows from both sides illustrates full preservation and missing-side NULLs.')
add(50,'''Suppose a reporting result contains North amount 100, South amount 100, and West amount 30. Without ORDER BY, its output order is unspecified. A stakeholder’s expectation that the biggest amounts appear first must be stated in the query.

Write:
SELECT Branch, Amount FROM ReportRows ORDER BY Amount DESC, Branch ASC;

Amount DESC puts larger amounts first. Branch ASC breaks the 100 tie, so the example order is North, South, West. The second sort column matters only when the first values tie. ASC is the default when a direction is omitted.

For the NULL ordering behavior stated in this brief, NULL values sort last with ASC and first with DESC unless overridden. To place missing amounts last in a descending report, use:
SELECT Branch, Amount FROM ReportRows ORDER BY Amount DESC NULLS LAST, Branch ASC;

If an East row has NULL Amount, that explicit placement puts it after the non-NULL amounts. Treat the stated default as the documented SQL behavior used here, not a universal assertion about every database engine.

The practical result is an ordering rule a report reader can understand: primary comparison, direction, tie-breaker, and where missing values belong. A consistent-looking order in one earlier result is not a substitute for specifying it.''','''Observed output order is not a guarantee without ORDER BY. Also, descending does not automatically put NULL last under the documented default here. State NULLS FIRST or NULLS LAST when the report needs explicit placement.''','NULL defaults are source/dialect-specific; do not generalize them to all SQL engines.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Sorting equal values with a tie-breaker and moving NULL placement makes ordering rules concrete.')
add(51,'''Suppose hypothetical Orders totals are North 100, South 80, and West 80. To report the top two branches, compute each group’s metric, sort it descending, include a tie-breaker, and limit the output:
SELECT Branch, SUM(Amount) AS total FROM Orders GROUP BY Branch ORDER BY total DESC, Branch LIMIT 2;
The result is North 100 and South 80. The Branch ordering decides between the tied 80 totals; the limit alone would not establish a predictable top-two result.

For period comparison, suppose PeriodMetrics contains unique periods 1, 2, and 3 with Metric 10, 15, and 8. Write:
SELECT Period, Metric, LAG(Metric) OVER (ORDER BY Period) FROM PeriodMetrics ORDER BY Period;
The previous-row values are NULL, 10, and 15. The first row has no previous row and defaults to NULL.

For a running total, write:
SELECT Period, SUM(Metric) OVER (ORDER BY Period ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) FROM PeriodMetrics ORDER BY Period;
The totals are 10, 25, and 33. The frame runs from the first row through the current row. These are three different reporting patterns: ranked limited output, previous-row comparison, and cumulative total. Keep the unique period ordering in this example visible rather than implying a previous-row value is always the previous calendar period when data has gaps.''','''LIMIT alone does not determine which rows come first. LAG retrieves the previous ordered row, while the specified SUM frame accumulates rows through the current one. Do not describe these distinct calculations as if they all simply return a grouped total.''','Use unique ordered periods in examples; explain only the supplied window patterns.','HIGH','annotated_code','Running ranked, previous-row, and cumulative queries against small tables makes their different result states visible.')
add(52,'''Imagine receiving a dispatch-orders source with OrderID, Amount, and an unnecessary note column. You need to connect and shape the data before loading it into Power BI Desktop, and Amount should have a type appropriate to its numeric information.

Use Home > Get data to select the connector and connect to the source. Choose Transform Data to open Power Query Editor. There, use column commands to change Amount’s data type and remove the note column that is unnecessary for this example. The transformations are recorded as applied steps.

Inspect the intended shape before loading: the example needs the order identifier and amount, with the correct type for Amount. The applied steps record the transformations rather than requiring you to describe them only from memory. Then use Close & Apply to apply the changes and load the shaped data.

The practical distinction is between connecting to a source and shaping the data before loading. The path teaches tool use from scratch, so keep the connector, editor, and final apply action explicit. This example does not yet create relationships, measures, or report visuals. Its output is loaded, shaped data for later work, not a completed dashboard merely because a connection succeeded.''','''Get data is the connection step, not the whole transformation workflow. Transform Data opens the editor, and Close & Apply applies and loads the shaped result. Recorded applied steps are transformations, not a substitute for checking the intended columns and types.''','Do not introduce DAX, BI relationships, or report canvas commands.','HIGH','screen_demo','The connector, Power Query column commands, applied steps, and Close & Apply are best understood as a tool workflow.')
add(53,'''Suppose a hypothetical Tableau report needs Customers and Orders from a source. Start in the Connect pane, choose the appropriate connector, and provide that source’s connection information. Connection details depend on the source, so this example does not invent a universal login or file format.

For a physical join, drag the first table to the canvas, double-click it, and then drag the second table into the join canvas. In the join dialog, choose the join type and matching fields. If the question requires all customers, place and preserve the named side appropriately; inner keeps matches, left or right preserves its named side, and full outer preserves both.

If the workflow uses an extract, select Extract on the Data Source page and configure it before saving. An extract is a saved snapshot for analysis, not a claim that it always shows the newest source state.

The useful output is a connection and prepared data arrangement suited to the reporting question. Keep the physical-join canvas step explicit: dragging tables without explaining the double-click entry to that canvas leaves a beginner without the named workflow. The example ends at preparation, not at worksheet charts or dashboard placement.''','''An extract is a saved snapshot, so do not describe it as inherently live. Also preserve the distinction between connecting, creating a physical join in its canvas, and selecting an extract. Those are different preparation choices and actions.''','Avoid conflating physical joins with logical relationship modeling.','HIGH','screen_demo','Navigating Connect, the physical-join canvas, the join dialog, and Extract benefits from an annotated tool demonstration.')
add(54,'''Imagine an operations team wants to improve dispatch preparation. Before defining the desired result, record the present situation as a baseline. In this hypothetical assessment, dispatch information is kept in separate records, clarification calls occur before preparation, and one branch uses a different handoff from another.

Put detailed current-state notes in a structured current-state column. Organize them by business area: instruction access, handoffs, and dispatch preparation. That organization makes later comparison possible without mixing every observation into one vague statement such as “the process is inefficient.”

For instruction access, the example note might be “Staff consult a separate sheet for current instructions.” For handoffs, it might describe who supplies amended information. If the scenario provides a measured preparation time, record it with its context. Do not invent a baseline number merely because a later target will need comparison.

The output is a description of the present business situation arranged by the areas being assessed. It is not yet a list of desired changes. Keep an observed condition, a proposed improvement, and a judgment about success separate. This way a reader can see what current situation the subsequent goals and gaps refer to, rather than having to reconstruct the baseline from the recommendation.''','''Current-state assessment is not a wish list. A note saying “use one instruction record” describes a desired change, not the current situation. Record the baseline in the current-state column and organize it by the assessed business areas.''','Stay with structured baseline notes; do not assume measurements absent from the situation.')
add(55,'''Suppose the current-state assessment records lengthy dispatch preparation and unclear instruction access. Future-state vision states the objectives and situation the business wants to achieve, with a business outcome and measurable success target.

For this hypothetical effort, a desired objective could be “Dispatch staff prepare orders with current instructions, with preparation completed within five minutes in the stated operating conditions.” The five-minute target is an example business decision, not a standard for every dispatch operation. It gives the desired state more substance than “make dispatch better.”

Write desired objectives in the desired-state column beside the corresponding business areas. The instruction-access objective belongs alongside the instruction-access baseline; the preparation target belongs alongside the preparation baseline. Keeping that alignment visible lets readers compare like areas rather than comparing a broad aspiration with an unrelated current note.

The useful output is a future situation described in terms of business outcome and success target. It does not need to name a particular tool as the vision. “Purchase software” states an action, while “staff have current instructions and meet the preparation target” describes what the business wants to achieve. Keep the target’s conditions visible so its meaning is not lost in a slogan.''','''A future-state vision is not merely a named solution or a favorable adjective. State the desired business outcome with a measurable success target. It is intended future behavior, not evidence that the business has already reached that state.''','Do not turn the desired target into an unsupported universal service level.')
add(56,'''Imagine the current-state column says dispatch preparation takes twelve minutes under the assessed conditions, while the desired-state column sets a five-minute target under the same conditions. The specific time difference is seven minutes. Another current note says staff consult two instruction sources, while the desired state calls for current instructions available in one place.

Compare each current description with its corresponding desired objective. List the specific differences keeping the business from the goal: the preparation-time difference and the information-access difference in this hypothetical case. These are more precise than writing only “there is a dispatch gap.”

Keep the comparison tied to the descriptions supplied. If the current-state record contains no preparation-time measurement, do not calculate a numerical difference by inventing one. You can still identify the documented qualitative difference and preserve any missing information as a question.

The output is a gap list grounded in current and desired states. It does not itself prove the cause of every gap or choose the solution. The immediate analytical task is to make the difference explicit enough that stakeholders can see what has to change. The example’s numbers and one-place objective illustrate that comparison, not a claim about a real organization.''','''A gap is the difference between current and desired states, not simply a complaint or a proposed fix. “Buy a new system” does not identify the difference. Compare corresponding descriptions and state what separates the business from its goal.''','Do not infer root causes or guaranteed solutions from a gap alone.')
add(57,'''Suppose an order-service team is assessing a business situation. Its experienced dispatch staff are an internal advantage, while fragmented instruction records are an internal limitation. A new external delivery partnership may create a favorable possibility, while an external competitor’s improved service may threaten success.

Create four labeled quadrants: Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, and Threats. Place experienced staff under Strengths and fragmented records under Weaknesses. Place the partnership possibility under Opportunities and the competitor factor under Threats. These are hypothetical factors selected to make the two distinctions clear.

For each placement, ask two questions: is the factor internal or external, and favorable or unfavorable? Strong staff and the partnership are both favorable, but one is internal and the other external. Fragmentation and the competitor factor are both unfavorable, but they also belong on different sides of that distinction.

The useful result is an organized view of the situation rather than an unclassified list of positive and negative comments. Do not classify solely by whether a factor sounds important. A favorable external possibility belongs under Opportunities even if the team hopes to benefit from it internally. Keep the classification tied to the factor being described.''','''Strengths and opportunities are not interchangeable synonyms for positives; internal versus external distinguishes them. The same distinction separates weaknesses from threats. A four-quadrant layout helps only when each factor is placed using both dimensions.''','Avoid scoring SWOT factors or implying SWOT proves causation.','LOW','slides_with_signaling','A simple labeled four-quadrant visual is enough; motion would mostly repeat the classifications.')
add(58,'''Imagine prioritizing requirements for an initial delivery-instruction release. Define that scope before assigning MoSCoW categories. In this hypothetical scope, seeing current instructions is essential: without it, the solution would not work for its stated purpose, so the group assigns Must have.

An amendment indicator is important but the solution can still work without it, so it is a Should have in this example. A convenience color choice is desirable, nonessential, and has a smaller omission impact, so it is a Could have. A vehicle-purchasing feature is not needed within this scope, so it is Will not have. The same labels are not universal classifications of those features; they depend on the agreed scope and importance.

Discuss each item’s importance and assign the category the group agrees fits. Keep the reasoning attached to the category so Must have is not simply a synonym for “a stakeholder wants it strongly.” Should have and Could have both allow a working solution without the item, but the described importance and omission impact differ.

The practical output is scope-specific grouping that distinguishes essential work from important, desirable, or excluded work. Will not have refers to what is not needed within the prioritization scope; it need not be rewritten as a claim that the organization must never consider that item.''','''MoSCoW categories are not just four levels of enthusiasm. Must have is essential to a working or useful solution in the stated scope. Will not have is scope-specific, not necessarily a permanent ban. Define the scope and discuss importance before assigning labels.''','Keep “Will not have” terminology from the brief; no unsupported percentage allocations.')
add(59,'''Suppose user evidence about an order service shows several different satisfaction patterns. Users expect their current instructions to be accurate and are dissatisfied when they are missing; providing them adds little extra satisfaction. That pattern fits a Must-be or basic feature.

Evidence might show that progressively quicker instruction access produces more satisfaction: that is a Performance pattern. An unexpected helpful suggestion could delight users while its absence goes unnoticed: that is an Attractive or excitement pattern. If users remain neutral as a feature’s functionality changes, it is Indifferent.

Classify using evidence about satisfaction and functionality, not the BA’s personal excitement about an idea. In this hypothetical case, the same design team might find a decorative feature exciting while user evidence shows neutrality. The evidence would support Indifferent rather than Attractive.

The useful output is a discussion of how functionality relates to satisfaction. These categories do not state delivery priority by themselves. A feature being expected is a satisfaction pattern, while a separate prioritization decision asks what belongs in the delivery scope. Keep the concept focused on the supported patterns rather than inventing a Kano questionnaire or additional categories not present in the brief.''','''Attractive means it can delight users who did not expect it; it does not mean the team likes the feature. Basic features can cause dissatisfaction when absent even though their presence adds little satisfaction. Preserve that asymmetry instead of treating all features as linear satisfaction gains.''','Include Indifferent; avoid adding unsupported Kano categories or survey procedures.','HIGH','diagram_animation','Contrasting expected, improving, surprising, and neutral satisfaction patterns benefits from visual curves or changing states.')
add(60,'''Imagine comparing two hypothetical instruction-access options on clarity and implementation fit. Assign importance weights 0.6 to clarity and 0.4 to fit. Use a consistent scale where higher scores are better. Option A scores 4 and 2; Option B scores 3 and 4.

Multiply each score by its criterion weight and add the products. A totals 4 × 0.6 + 2 × 0.4 = 3.2. B totals 3 × 0.6 + 4 × 0.4 = 3.4. B ranks higher under these chosen criteria, weights, and scores.

The highest total is typically the best choice under the chosen setup, not a universal fact that B is best in every context. The weights represent importance, and the scores express performance on the consistent scale. Record them so another reader can understand the comparison rather than seeing only the final total.

The practical output is an explicit weighted comparison of options. If a stakeholder challenges the recommendation, return to the criteria, importance weights, and scores. Do not defend 3.4 as if it were independent of those choices. The example weights sum to one, but this brief does not make that normalization a mandatory rule for every weighted matrix.''','''Adding unweighted scores is not the weighted-total calculation. Multiply each criterion score by its importance weight first. Also keep the qualification: the highest total is typically best under the chosen criteria and weights, not proof of an objectively best solution in all circumstances.''','Preserve “typically”; do not prescribe weight normalization as mandatory.','HIGH','data_table_demo','A worked matrix can show score-by-weight products and how they combine into option totals.')
add(61,'''Suppose stakeholders are choosing between two instruction-access solutions. Define shared criteria before comparing them: instruction clarity and fit with the agreed operating constraints. For this hypothetical assessment, use weights 0.6 and 0.4 and a common score scale.

Solution A scores 4 for clarity and 2 for fit, giving 4 × 0.6 + 2 × 0.4 = 3.2. Solution B scores 3 and 4, giving 3 × 0.6 + 4 × 0.4 = 3.4. Compare the totals and recommend B on this assessment, explaining that its stronger fit outweighs A’s clarity advantage under the selected weights.

The recommendation should point back to the criteria and scores. “We prefer B” omits the analytical basis; “B totals 3.4 under the agreed clarity and fit assessment” makes that basis visible. The two totals are hypothetical and depend on the chosen assessment, not a claim about real products.

The useful BA output is a reasoned solution recommendation, not only a completed matrix. State what was compared, how importance affected the scores, and how the resulting totals support the recommendation. Keep candidate solutions on the same criteria rather than awarding one option points for an advantage the other option was never assessed against.''','''A preferred option does not become a supported recommendation merely because it is given a score. Evaluate every candidate against the same criteria and weights, calculate the totals, and explain the recommendation in those terms rather than using an unstructured preference.''','Avoid claims that scores guarantee implementation success.','HIGH','data_table_demo','A comparison table can link criterion scores, weighted totals, and the reason for a recommendation.')
add(62,'''Imagine a business case for improving delivery-instruction access. Describe the dispatch problem, possible solutions, and expected benefits and costs. For this hypothetical one-year assessment, estimated monetary benefits are 18,000 and relevant project costs total 12,000, including implementation and other relevant costs rather than only a headline purchase price.

Projected net monetary return is benefits minus costs: 18,000 − 12,000 = 6,000. Percentage ROI is that net return divided by investment cost, times 100%: 6,000 / 12,000 × 100% = 50%. The 6,000 amount and 50% percentage describe different things. Gross benefits of 18,000 are not the net return.

A positive projected monetary return means the estimated benefits exceed cost over the stated period. Report the one-year horizon and the assumptions behind the estimates; this is not a promised financial outcome. If another alternative is compared, use costs and monetary benefits for the same evaluation period, including relevant direct and indirect effects.

Present the recommendation with its supporting financial assessment in the business case. Do not let the calculation replace the problem and option descriptions. The useful output explains why the initiative is being considered and what estimated return supports the recommendation, with the costs, period, and assumptions visible to readers.''','''ROI’s numerator is net monetary return, not gross savings. Its denominator is investment cost, not benefits. A positive projection is an estimate under stated assumptions and a time horizon, not a promise. Net return in money and percentage ROI must not be labeled interchangeably.''','Keep cost completeness, same-period comparisons, and estimates explicit; no unsupported NPV or payback formulas.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Tracing costs and benefits into net return and percentage ROI makes denominators, units, and time horizons clear.')
add(63,'''Suppose two instruction-access options differ in cost and clarity. Cost is measured in money, while clarity uses a rating. Adding 8,000 directly to a clarity score of 4 would mix incompatible units, so define a common rating scale first.

For this hypothetical matrix, clarity already uses 1 to 5, with higher ratings better. Define cost ratings specifically: 8,000 maps to 5 and 12,000 maps to 3 for the example options, because lower cost is better under the chosen scoring definitions. Apply each criterion’s definition consistently to every option. Assign weights 0.4 to cost and 0.6 to clarity.

Option A has cost rating 5 and clarity 3, totaling 5 × 0.4 + 3 × 0.6 = 3.8. Option B has cost rating 3 and clarity 5, totaling 3 × 0.4 + 5 × 0.6 = 4.2. Compare those totals for the ranking.

The useful output makes criteria, importance, criterion-specific scoring definitions, and weighted totals explicit. The invented mappings are part of this assessment, not a universal monetary conversion. Higher ratings must mean better performance across the criteria, including cost, so the weighted sum has a consistent interpretation.''','''Weights do not make incompatible raw units directly comparable. Map performance to a common rating scale with criterion-specific definitions first. Also reverse the scoring direction where needed so higher ratings mean better performance rather than accidentally rewarding higher cost.''','No invented normalization formula; use explicitly defined sample ratings.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Mapping different units to a common scale before calculating weighted totals benefits from a worked matrix.')
add(64,'''Imagine five observed dispatch-preparation times in minutes: 2, 3, 3, 4, and 18. The mean is the sum divided by the number of observations: 30 / 5 = 6 minutes. The ordered middle value is 3, so the median is 3 minutes. The most frequent value is also 3, so the mode is 3 minutes.

The extreme 18-minute observation affects the mean more strongly than the median. In this skewed sample, the median can better represent a typical value than the mean. Do not hide the extreme observation; explain why the two center summaries differ.

For an even-sized example, 2, 3, 4, and 9 has median (3 + 4) / 2 = 3.5. For mode, 2, 2, 4, and 4 has two most frequent values, so both 2 and 4 are modes. These examples apply the definitions rather than assuming every dataset has a single mode or a median that is an observed value.

The practical BA task is to interpret a center measure in the context of the business data. A report saying “average preparation time is 6 minutes” should be clear that it uses the mean. The median answers a different center question and is more robust to extreme observations.''','''Mean, median, and mode are not interchangeable names for one number. Order values before finding the median, average the two middle values when the count is even, and allow multiple modes. An extreme value can pull the mean away from the median.''','Do not introduce inferential statistics or automatic outlier removal.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Ordering values and changing an extreme observation makes center measures and their sensitivity visible.')
add(65,'''Suppose dispatch-preparation times are 2, 4, and 6 minutes. Their mean is 4. The range is 6 − 2 = 4 minutes. For the sample variance, subtract the mean from each observation to get -2, 0, and 2; square these deviations to get 4, 0, and 4; then sum to 8.

Divide by n − 1, which is 2 for three observations: variance is 8 / 2 = 4 square minutes. Standard deviation is the square root of that variance, or 2 minutes. The different units matter: variance is squared, while standard deviation uses the original minutes and describes typical distance from the mean.

Range only uses the smallest and largest values. A second sample, 2, 4, 4, 4, and 6, has the same range, but its variance is 8 / 4 = 2 square minutes and standard deviation is about 1.41 minutes. The added central observations change the spread calculation without changing the extremes.

The practical reporting point is that range alone does not describe how the rest of the data are spread. State which measure you use and its units. The variance formula here is the sample form with n − 1; keep that denominator instead of silently switching to another convention.''','''Do not divide this sample-variance calculation by n: the stated denominator is n − 1. Standard deviation is not the variance itself, and it is not in squared units. Equal ranges also do not prove equal spread among all observations.''','Explicitly label sample variance; examples have at least two observations.','HIGH','data_table_demo','Tracing deviations, squares, denominator, and square root makes the calculation and units concrete.')
add(66,'''Imagine a backlog story saying, “As a dispatcher, I want all order-management capabilities, so that I can do my work.” It is broad enough that the team cannot clearly discuss its scope or finish it within an iteration. Refinement breaks oversized items into smaller, more precise stories.

For this hypothetical case, rewrite one story around seeing current instructions and another around finding orders by customer. Each needs a persona’s need and purpose, not merely a technical layer. Check each rewritten story with INVEST. Independent asks whether it can be implemented without requiring the other first and without conceptual overlap. Negotiable asks whether it captures the essential need while details remain open to collaboration. Valuable asks whether it delivers a customer-valued result.

Estimable asks whether the team understands it well enough to approximate the work for ranking and scheduling. Small asks whether it fits within an iteration. Testable asks whether the customer can describe how to verify the result. If the rewritten instruction story still says only “make access better,” clarify it until a test can be described. If it cannot fit, split it further.

The output is not just more story cards. It is a set of clearer, smaller stories checked for all six qualities. The example split is a candidate to assess, not a guarantee of independence or size without the team’s checks.''','''Splitting into “database,” “interface,” and “testing” pieces does not by itself satisfy Valuable: each split story should describe a customer-valued result. Nor does using the story sentence format prove INVEST is met. Check each quality and reword or rewrite failures.''','Include all INVEST meanings; do not assume an invented split is automatically independent.')
add(67,'''Suppose the order-service backlog contains a broad instruction-access item, a clearer customer-search item, and an outdated vague item called “reporting improvements.” The product backlog is an emergent ordered list of needed product-improvement work, not a fixed archive of every initial idea.

Refine unclear or oversized items. For the instruction item, clarify the description and break it into smaller, more precise items if needed. For the vague reporting item, establish the intended detail before treating it as ready. Add detail such as description, order, and size, then keep the backlog ordered.

Items that can be completed within one iteration are ready for selection; refinement usually provides the transparency needed for that judgment. Do not label an oversized item ready merely because it appears near the top. Conversely, refinement is not just changing order: it also improves detail and precision.

The useful BA contribution is clearer items and an understandable ordered backlog. This example does not give the BA authority to impose the order or size; those accountabilities are taught later. Keep the present explanation focused on the nature of the backlog and the work of clarification, splitting, detail, and ordering.''','''A backlog is not simply an unordered requirements list. Refinement also does more than re-rank items: it makes them smaller and more precise with relevant details. High placement alone does not establish that an item fits within an iteration.''','Preserve “usually” for refinement transparency; avoid assigning later Scrum accountabilities prematurely.')
add(68,'''Imagine planned order-service work includes current-instruction access, a customer-search improvement, and broader multi-branch reporting. A roadmap sequences planned work against goals rather than presenting disconnected features.

For this hypothetical roadmap, place instruction access in Now to support the dispatch-preparation goal, customer search in Next to support faster order finding, and multi-branch reporting in Later to support branch comparison. Track known dependencies as part of the sequence. If later reporting depends on agreed branch information, make that connection visible instead of treating the three labels as independent wish lists.

Now, Next, and Later are an example sequence, not fixed dates promised by this concept. Link each initiative to its goal so a stakeholder can understand why it occupies that part of the roadmap. Then review the sequence as priorities and information change.

If new information changes which work supports the immediate goal, reconsider the initial placement. The useful output is goal-linked planned sequencing with dependencies and revision, not a permanent commitment disguised as a diagram. The example initiatives are invented for the exercise; their particular order is not a universal rule for BA delivery.''','''A roadmap is not just a list of features with three headings. The sequence should connect to goals and track dependencies. Also, the initial sequence is not fixed: review it as priorities and information change rather than claiming the first roadmap cannot be revised.''','Do not invent scheduling authority or fixed durations for Now/Next/Later.','MEDIUM','slides_with_signaling','A goal-linked sequence with dependency cues can make the relationship between initiatives and phases easier to follow.')
add(69,'''Suppose a story says dispatchers want to see current instructions so they can prepare the correct order. Acceptance criteria state the conditions used to judge whether this particular story is satisfied. Discuss what completing it means instead of relying only on the story’s short wording.

For this hypothetical case, the stakeholders agree that selecting an existing order displays its current instructions, and selecting an unknown identifier displays a not-found result. Make those acceptance conditions testable and document the shared understanding. “The lookup is good” does not describe a clear completion condition.

During refinement, add detail that makes the backlog item more precise and understandable. If stakeholders disagree about what “current” means, clarify that point before leaving the criterion as if everyone interpreted it identically. The example conditions are agreed choices for this story, not a complete checklist for all instruction-access systems.

The useful output is story-specific completion understanding that can be checked. It should connect directly to the desired behavior rather than becoming a broad quality slogan. Keep the conditions in the shared record so later discussion is about the documented understanding, not a different person’s recollection of what was meant.''','''Acceptance criteria belong to a particular story. They are not a substitute for clarifying what that story means, and vague positive wording does not make them testable. Discuss and document the shared completion conditions instead of assuming the title is sufficient.''','Do not introduce Definition of Done early; focus on testable story conditions.')
add(70,'''Imagine an operations manager receiving a report about dispatch delays. A page of observations alone leaves the reader to find the main conclusion. Structure the report for its readers and purpose.

For this hypothetical report, a headline could state “Instruction clarification is the main issue identified in dispatch preparation.” Highlights then summarize the relevant findings, and supporting detail explains the evidence available in the exercise. The headline is an example report conclusion based on assumed findings, not a claim that clarification is always the main delay.

This layering serves different reading needs. A scanning reader can find the key message; a reader needing more can continue into highlights and detail. Select information that serves the report’s purpose and use the audience’s required level of detail rather than including every note simply because it exists.

The practical output is not a shorter report at any cost. It is an organized report whose key message is prominent and whose supporting information remains available. If the purpose is to communicate the dispatch finding, unrelated observations about office equipment distract from that purpose. Keep enough relevant detail for the intended reader without assuming findings will explain themselves.''','''Supporting detail does not remove the need for a clear key message. Conversely, a headline alone is not the same as a structured report for readers who need evidence. Use headlines, highlights, and relevant detail rather than choosing between a slogan and an unorganized data dump.''','Keep example headline explicitly based on hypothetical findings; do not invent evidence.')
add(71,'''Suppose the same instruction-access finding must be explained to dispatch managers and to technical colleagues. The audience determines the appropriate detail, terminology, and communication approach.

For managers, a headline might say “Missing instruction matches leave some orders unresolved,” followed by the operational meaning. For technical readers familiar with the query, supporting detail can identify the relevant matching fields and the result behavior. Both explanations should preserve the same finding; changing audience is not permission to change the evidence.

Use the audience’s language. If an unfamiliar relationship needs explanation, a familiar analogy may help: a customer identifier can be compared to a reference that points to a customer record. Use the analogy to clarify the concept, not replace its actual meaning. Keep the main ideas clear in headlines and preserve detail for readers who need it.

The practical skill is selecting how much explanation and which terms serve these readers. A nontechnical audience does not require a different conclusion, and a technical audience does not require every possible implementation detail. The example communications are choices for this hypothetical report, not fixed scripts for everyone with those job titles.''','''Audience adaptation changes detail and language, not the supported facts. Unexplained jargon can obscure the main idea, while deleting all supporting detail can leave readers unable to understand it. Preserve clear headlines and the detail appropriate to their needs.''','Avoid implying all technical or nontechnical audiences have identical knowledge.')
add(72,'''Imagine presenting a recommendation about delivery-instruction access. Instead of listing disconnected numbers, build a narrative that makes the findings meaningful to the stakeholder audience.

For this hypothetical presentation, the opening headline states the dispatch problem, the next highlight explains the relevant instruction-access finding, and the recommendation follows with its supporting detail. The exact sequence is an example structure, not a compulsory slide count. Use audience-appropriate language to connect the finding to what you recommend.

A reader who scans the headlines should still understand the main ideas. A participant who needs more should be able to follow the highlights and supporting detail. If a chart or table is included, explain its role in the finding rather than assuming the data alone carries the narrative.

The useful output is a presentation whose evidence and recommendation are intelligibly connected for this audience. The hypothetical recommendation must be supported by the assumed findings; do not add persuasive claims merely because they sound compelling. Keep the explanation focused on narrative, layering, language, and connection to the recommendation, not on visual effects or a mandatory presentation template.''','''A bare list of data is not necessarily a meaningful presentation. Also, narrative does not mean inventing a more dramatic conclusion. Connect findings to the recommendation with clear headlines, highlights, and appropriate supporting detail for the audience.''','No unsupported slide counts or persuasive devices.','MEDIUM','slides_with_signaling','A signaled presentation example can show how headlines, evidence, and recommendation form a coherent narrative.')
add(73,'''Suppose delivery-instruction requirements are ready for stakeholder review. Plan the walkthrough around the documented goal, scope, requirements, assumptions, and open questions. Its purpose is to give stakeholders and approvers an opportunity to confirm priorities and agree what will be delivered.

In this hypothetical walkthrough, review the instruction-display requirement and its acceptance conditions, then make the unresolved meaning of “current” visible. Record decisions in the shared requirements document instead of letting meeting discussion become the only source of agreement.

Close the agenda by asking designated approvers to approve the reviewed requirements or state outstanding conditions. Record the approval outcome, the approvers, and any unresolved conditions in the shared decision log. For example, the record might state that approval remains conditional on clarifying the instruction-update rule. That is different from recording unconditional approval.

The useful output is a visible agreement and decision record. A demonstration can support discussion, but the fact that a feature was shown does not itself record approval. Keep the reviewed scope and unresolved questions connected to the decision so later readers can understand what was actually agreed and what remains open.''','''Attendance or a successful demonstration is not an approval record. Ask designated approvers for approval or outstanding conditions, then record the outcome and approvers. Do not rewrite a conditional outcome as full agreement merely because the walkthrough ended.''','Keep explicit approval closeout; avoid inventing approval authority for the BA.','MEDIUM','talking_head_with_visual_support','A walkthrough example can show the review agenda and explicit approval-or-conditions closeout.')
add(74,'''Imagine defining the boundary of an order service. A context diagram represents the system as a single central element, with external entities outside its boundary. The immediate question is what is inside this system and what exchanges information with it.

For this hypothetical diagram, draw “Order service” centrally. Place “Dispatcher” and “External delivery service” around it as external entities. Label directional arrows with the exchanges: a dispatcher sends an order-selection request to the system; the system returns instructions; the system sends a dispatch request to the delivery service. These are example exchanges, not mandatory integrations.

Define the system’s purpose and boundary before placing the entities. Do not put the external delivery service inside merely because it communicates with the order service. The directional labels help the reader distinguish what flows into and out of the central system.

The useful output is a boundary-and-exchange view, not a detailed internal process. The central system stays one element here. That makes the context clear without introducing the internal tasks or screens that may implement it. Read the diagram back as purpose, boundary, external actors or connected systems, and labeled exchanges.''','''A context diagram does not expand the system into all its internal activities. It represents the system as one element and shows external entities and exchanges. A connected system is not automatically inside the boundary simply because there is an arrow to it.''','Avoid internal decomposition and unsupported formal data-flow notation.','HIGH','whiteboard_drawing','Drawing a boundary, external entities, and labeled directional exchanges clarifies system scope.')
add(75,'''Suppose the top-level goal is “Prepare an order for dispatch.” Functional decomposition represents that larger goal hierarchically through major tasks and smaller parts.

Draw the goal at the top. In this hypothetical decomposition, its major parts are “Identify order,” “Obtain instructions,” and “Prepare dispatch information.” Beneath Obtain instructions, place “Find current instructions” and “Clarify missing information.” These are illustrative parts of the goal, not a universal dispatch procedure.

Choose the number of levels according to the complexity and detail needed. If the analysis only needs the main parts, stop at that useful level. If a part requires more detail for the purpose, break it into relevant subparts. Do not keep adding levels simply to make every branch the same depth.

The useful output is a hierarchy showing how a larger goal breaks into constituent tasks. It is not necessarily execution order: placing two parts side by side shows that they belong under the same parent, not that they occur simultaneously or in a particular sequence. Keep the parent-child meaning explicit so the learner does not confuse this decomposition with the process flows taught earlier.''','''A hierarchy of parts is not automatically a sequence of steps. Read lower nodes as subparts of the goal or task above them. The number of levels depends on needed complexity and detail, not an unsupported fixed requirement for every diagram.''','Do not infer sequencing from the hierarchy; avoid a mandatory number of levels.','HIGH','whiteboard_drawing','Expanding a goal into major tasks and subparts makes hierarchical meaning distinct from process sequence.')
add(76,'''Imagine a spreadsheet with order amounts in A2:A4 containing 10, 20, and 0, and a factor in B1 containing 0.1. A formula starts with =. To apply the factor to A2, write =A2*$B$1 in C2, then copy it to C3. The relative A2 becomes A3, while the absolute $B$1 stays fixed.

A relative reference such as A1 changes with the copied position. $A$1 fixes both row and column; $A1 fixes only the column, and A$1 fixes only the row. For example, copying a formula from C2 to D3 shifts a relative A1 to B2, keeps $A$1 unchanged, shifts $A1 to $A2, and shifts A$1 to B$1. Inspect the destination formula instead of assuming every reference moves identically.

For the sample amounts, =SUM(A2:A4) returns 30, =AVERAGE(A2:A4) returns 10, and =COUNT(A2:A4) returns 3 because all three cells are numeric. AVERAGE includes zero. If A4 were empty rather than zero, the average of the referenced numbers would be 15, not 10; text and empty cells are ignored in that range calculation.

The practical skill is reading both the calculation and the reference behavior. Fixed references preserve an intended shared input while relative references move to the corresponding source cell when copied.''','''A dollar sign fixes the part it precedes, not always the whole reference. Also, zero and an empty cell do not have the same effect on AVERAGE: zero is included, while empty cells and text in the referenced range are ignored.''','Trace copying in both directions; no unintroduced conditional or lookup syntax.','HIGH','spreadsheet_demo','Copying formulas and inspecting relative, absolute, mixed references, and average results requires visible cell changes.')
add(77,'''Suppose a worksheet has columns Branch and Amount, with North amounts 40 and 60 and South amount 30. A PivotTable can calculate, summarize, and analyze those rows to reveal comparisons rather than displaying only the original records.

Select the source data cells, choose Insert > PivotTable, select where to place the report, and choose OK. In the PivotTable Fields pane, check Branch and Amount. The nonnumeric Branch field is added to Rows by default, and the numeric Amount field to Values. Fields can be moved by dragging them to the desired area.

Read the areas deliberately. Rows creates row labels on the left, Columns creates column labels at the top, Values displays summarized numeric values, and Filters provides report filters above the PivotTable. In this hypothetical report, using Branch in Rows and Amount summarized by a total in Values gives North 100 and South 30. That illustrates the intended summary; inspect the numeric summary rather than assuming every possible source field produces the same calculation.

The useful output is a summarized view built from field placement. Moving a field changes its role in the report, so explain the placement in business terms: Branch defines the comparison groups and Amount provides the numeric summary.''','''Checking a field uses its default placement; that placement is not a permanent rule. Drag fields to the area serving the question. Rows, Columns, Filters, and Values are different roles, and a numeric summary should not be mistaken for an original record.''','Do not invent refresh behavior or assume all numeric-looking data are typed as numeric.','HIGH','spreadsheet_demo','Creating the PivotTable and moving fields through areas makes report layout and summarized values concrete.')
add(78,'''Imagine a customer list with identifiers 101 and 102 in A2:A3 and names North Stores and South Stores in B2:B3. D2 contains the identifier 102. A lookup retrieves the value associated with a matching key.

Write =XLOOKUP(D2,A2:A3,B2:B3) to return South Stores. Exact match is the default. With the same two-column list, =VLOOKUP(D2,A2:B3,2,FALSE) also returns South Stores: it finds an exact key in the first column and returns column 2 to its right.

Do not omit FALSE casually in this exact-key example. If VLOOKUP’s final argument is omitted or TRUE, it uses approximate matching and expects the first column sorted. That is a different lookup mode, not merely shorter spelling for the exact request.

Another exact form is =INDEX(B2:B3,MATCH(D2,A2:A3,0)). MATCH with 0 finds the exact key’s position, and INDEX returns the value at that position from the return range. In the sample, the key is second and the returned name is South Stores.

The useful BA task is joining a needed business label to a known key through the lookup ranges. Keep the ranges aligned in the example so the matching position corresponds to the intended returned value. This lesson does not invent duplicate-key or missing-key behavior beyond the brief.''','''VLOOKUP’s omitted final argument is not exact matching. For the supported exact pattern, use FALSE; approximate matching expects a sorted first column. XLOOKUP’s exact default and MATCH’s 0 also need to be read as part of the chosen lookup form.''','Do not invent missing-key handling or teach extra optional arguments.','HIGH','spreadsheet_demo','Tracing key positions and comparing three exact formulas with VLOOKUP mode choice makes lookups tangible.')
add(79,'''Suppose a spreadsheet labels order amounts using a hypothetical policy: amounts above 100 are High, amounts above 50 but not above 100 are Medium, and the rest are Low. A2 contains the amount to classify.

For a two-way decision, =IF(A2>100,"High","Other") returns High when the test is true and Other when it is false. For three ordered alternatives, use =IF(A2>100,"High",IF(A2>50,"Medium","Low")). The second test is reached only through the false alternative of the first.

The same policy can be written =IFS(A2>100,"High",A2>50,"Medium",TRUE,"Low"). IFS returns the result associated with the first true condition; the final TRUE supplies a default. With amounts 120, 75, and 40, the results are High, Medium, and Low. Exactly 100 is Medium and exactly 50 is Low because these tests use >, not an inclusive comparison.

If the IFS expression contains only the two amount tests and neither is true, it returns #N/A. The final TRUE avoids that no-true-condition case by providing the default. The useful skill is tracing ordered tests rather than assuming the formula evaluates all matching labels and combines them.''','''In IFS, the first true condition wins. Putting A2>50 before A2>100 would classify 120 as Medium in this policy. A final TRUE supplies a default; without any true condition, IFS returns #N/A rather than an invented blank or Low label.''','Trace boundaries and first-true ordering; no unintroduced AND/OR syntax.','HIGH','spreadsheet_demo','Changing input values and reordering tests makes nested alternatives and IFS first-true behavior visible.')
add(80,'''Imagine a worksheet with Branch in A1, TotalAmount in B1, and rows North 100 and South 30. The question is a category comparison, so a bar chart fits. A different sheet of amounts by month would support a line chart for a trend over time.

In Excel for the web, select A1:B3 and use Insert > Charts to choose a suitable type. Give the chart a clear title such as “Order amount by branch” and labels that make the comparison interpretable. The title is an example, not a required naming pattern.

Right-click the chart and choose Select Data. In Data > Setup, assign A2:A3 to Horizontal Axis > Horizontal Field and add B2:B3 under Vertical Axis > Add Field, checking that the category and value fields occupy the intended axes. Inspect the source range and plotted series in the chart task pane, correcting names or ranges as needed. Then use Format to check the chart title, axis titles, and displayed data labels.

The useful output is a chart whose type and field placement answer the business question. Selecting a range and accepting a first appearance is not the same as checking that the chart uses the intended categories and values.''','''A chart type should fit the question, not merely look attractive. Bars support category comparisons and lines support trends over time. Also inspect the plotted source and field placement; a clear title cannot fix a chart using the wrong range.''','Use Excel for the web commands exactly; do not substitute desktop-specific UI paths.','HIGH','spreadsheet_demo','Range selection, axis assignment, source checks, and formatting are an explicit spreadsheet workflow.')
add(81,'''Suppose an operations manager asks, “Which branch has the largest total order amount in this extract?” Clarify that the desired analysis is a branch-level total, not average order amount or a count of orders.

For this hypothetical source, North amounts are 40 and 60 and South amount is 30. Select the source table or range and insert a PivotTable. Place Branch in Rows and Amount in Values, then select the aggregation that answers the question: a total for this request. The resulting totals are North 100 and South 30. Columns can also structure the summary when the business question needs another grouping dimension, but this simple question does not require one.

Present the answer prominently: “North has the larger total in this extract: 100 compared with South’s 30.” Include enough supporting detail for the audience to understand what was summarized and which source the answer refers to. The stated result is for the hypothetical extract, not a claim about all business activity.

The useful output is a targeted answer supported by a relevant summary. Starting with a business question helps you choose grouping and aggregation; opening a spreadsheet does not itself define what the analysis should accomplish.''','''A count, average, and total answer different business questions. Do not accept a Values summary without checking the aggregation needed. Also, the report should state the answer clearly rather than make the reader infer it from a PivotTable alone.''','Keep reporting scoped to the supplied source; no unsupported causal claims.','HIGH','spreadsheet_demo','Building a question-specific pivot and checking its aggregation links tool use to the reported answer.')
add(82,'''Imagine the team is planning an iteration containing instruction-access work. Sprint Planning collaboratively establishes the iteration’s work, rather than receiving a commitment imposed from outside the Developers.

Review important backlog items together. For this hypothetical case, the instruction-display item has clear behavior and acceptance detail, while an amendment item still leaves the intended result unclear. Inspect candidate descriptions and refine uncertain items before selection. Select work that can be completed within the iteration.

Consider Developers' capacity and past performance in selection. A stakeholder’s strong preference does not replace that consideration. The BA can help clarify requirements and questions, while the planning discussion remains collaborative rather than becoming a BA-issued delivery promise.

The useful contribution is transparent candidate work with enough detail for the team to reason about selection. If an item cannot yet be understood, record and resolve the uncertainty instead of silently assuming its scope. The example does not prescribe how many items belong in the iteration; that decision depends on the collaborative assessment and Developers' capacity and past performance, not on a universal quota for business analysis work.''','''High priority does not by itself establish that all requested work fits. Selection considers Developers' capacity and past performance. Clarifying a requirement also does not authorize the BA or another external participant to impose the Developers' commitment.''','Respect developer selection considerations; no prescribed sprint item counts.')
add(83,'''Suppose the BA is helping clarify an instruction-access item while the Product Owner and Developers discuss delivery. Product Owner accountability includes maximizing product value and effective backlog management. Some backlog-management work can be delegated, but that does not transfer the accountability.

In this hypothetical collaboration, the BA drafts clearer item wording and brings stakeholder questions to the group. The Product Owner remains accountable for ordering. Developers size the work and decide how selected work will be performed. The BA’s useful contribution is clarification that helps these decisions, not taking them over.

If a stakeholder asks the BA to promise a size or dictate the implementation approach, distinguish the BA’s requirements contribution from Developers' responsibility. If the BA updates backlog detail under delegated work, distinguish doing that task from becoming accountable for product value and backlog management.

The practical mental model is collaboration with explicit boundaries. People can contribute to the same item while retaining different accountabilities and delivery decisions. Clear descriptions support collaboration, but they should not be presented as authority to impose order, size, or implementation on the people accountable for those decisions.''','''Delegating backlog-management work does not transfer the Product Owner’s accountability. Nor does preparing requirements make the BA responsible for sizing or deciding how selected work is performed. Collaborate on clarity while respecting ordering and delivery boundaries.''','Do not conflate BA contribution with Product Owner or Developer authority.')
add(84,'''The Daily Scrum is a 15-minute event for Developers focused on progress toward the Sprint Goal and an actionable plan. Other Scrum accountabilities participate as Developers when actively working on iteration backlog items. For example: “The instruction clarification blocks this goal-related item; I need coordination to resolve it, then continue the planned work.” Keep detailed replanning outside the event rather than extending it.''','''This is not a general status meeting for every stakeholder. Keep the focus on the goal and next work plan.''','Keep brief; no automatic BA attendance rule.')
add(85,'''Imagine stakeholders examining the iteration’s order-service outcome. The Sprint Review is a working session for inspecting that outcome and determining future adaptations, not merely a polished presentation.

For this hypothetical story, acceptance conditions say that selecting an existing order displays current instructions and an unknown identifier displays a not-found result. Compare the demonstrated behavior with those story-specific conditions. If the unknown-identifier case behaves differently, discuss that difference with stakeholders rather than relying on a general claim that the demo looked successful.

Use the inspection to discuss future adaptations. The conditions help judge whether the demonstrated behavior satisfies the particular need, while the review addresses the outcome with stakeholders. Keep the example’s conditions visible so participants can connect what they saw with what was expected.

The useful BA contribution is evidence-based comparison and discussion of differences. This concept does not make the review a substitute for every shared quality requirement or authorize presenting unfinished work; those boundaries belong to the later Definition of Done treatment. Here the emphasis is on outcome inspection, story-specific conditions, and collaborative adaptation rather than a one-way show.''','''A presentation of features alone does not capture the review’s working-session purpose. Compare behavior with the story’s acceptance conditions and discuss differences with stakeholders. A convincing demonstration is not evidence that every specified condition was met.''','Do not introduce Definition of Done rules before their concept; keep review focused on inspection and adaptation.','MEDIUM','talking_head_with_visual_support','A review example can show demonstrated behavior compared with conditions and discussed as future adaptations.')
add(86,'''Suppose the team completed an iteration but spent significant effort clarifying instruction wording late in the work. The retrospective plans ways to improve quality and effectiveness; it is not another stakeholder presentation of the product outcome.

Review what went well, what problems occurred, and how those problems were or were not solved. In this hypothetical discussion, clear customer examples worked well, while late uncertainty about “current instructions” interrupted work. Consider interactions, processes, and tools rather than treating the issue as only one person’s performance.

Identify a concrete change. The team might agree to clarify instruction examples during refinement and arrange to address that helpful improvement promptly. That is an example action chosen for this situation, not a mandatory retrospective technique.

The useful output is a specific improvement the team intends to address, grounded in its review. A record saying only “communicate better” leaves the change unclear. Keep the discussion connected to what happened and what can improve effectiveness, including whether earlier problems were actually solved. The focus is learning about the team’s way of working, not inventing a new product requirement simply because the team has held a meeting.''','''A retrospective is not just a list of complaints or a repeat of the Sprint Review. It examines quality and effectiveness and leads toward concrete improvements. Naming a problem without identifying a helpful change does not complete that purpose.''','Avoid unsupported retrospective ceremonies or mandatory voting methods.')
add(87,'''Imagine the problem statement is “Dispatch preparation is delayed by clarification calls.” Ask why those calls occurred. In this hypothetical causal chain, staff could not find current instructions. Ask why that happened: amended instructions remained in a separate record. Ask why they remained separate: the handoff did not identify where the current instruction record should be updated.

Write each answer and use it as the starting point for the next why question. The chain moves from the stated problem toward an underlying cause instead of repeatedly renaming “dispatch delay.” These answers are assumptions provided for the exercise, not a factual diagnosis of a real team’s process.

Five is a guide, not a required stopping point. Do not invent a fifth explanation merely to reach the technique’s name, and do not stop automatically at five if the chain has not reached an underlying cause. Keep the successive explanation visible so a reader can follow how the cause of the previous explanation is being examined.

The useful output is a recorded causal chain tied to the original problem. The technique is not a guarantee that the first plausible answer is the true cause. For this brief, the learner should grasp repeated why questions and the difference between an underlying explanation and a restated symptom.''','''“Why?” followed by the same symptom in different words does not extend the causal chain. Five is not a compulsory count. Avoid manufacturing answers to fill five lines or treating the technique’s name as proof that the fifth answer must be the root cause.''','All sample causal answers are hypothetical; do not present guesses as verified real causes.','MEDIUM','whiteboard_drawing','A drawn question-and-answer chain can show how each why extends the previous explanation.')
add(88,'''Suppose the specific problem is “Orders wait for instruction clarification before dispatch.” A fishbone diagram organizes possible causes for analysis rather than declaring every proposed cause proven.

For this hypothetical team discussion, select categories suited to the problem, such as information, handoffs, and tools. Write the problem statement and brainstorm potential causes. Place “amendments stored separately” under information, “unclear update responsibility” under handoffs, and “difficult instruction lookup” under tools. These category names and causes are example choices, not a mandatory universal set.

Use the branches to expose patterns in the proposed causes. Then analyze them and agree actions addressing the root cause. Do not jump from a busy branch to a causal conclusion without that analysis. The diagram’s organizational role helps the team see possibilities; the later analysis is what examines them.

The useful output is a structured cause discussion connected to a specific problem, followed by analyzed causes and agreed actions. A vague problem such as “everything is slow” gives the branches less focus than a concrete statement about waiting for clarification. Keep potential causes explicitly potential until they have been analyzed rather than labeling the diagram a proof of cause.''','''Putting a cause on a branch does not verify it. A fishbone diagram organizes possible causes, which still need analysis before actions address the root cause. The categories should suit the problem, not be treated as a fixed set every business must use.''','Do not add mandatory cause-category lists or infer cause from number of suggestions.','HIGH','whiteboard_drawing','Drawing category branches and placing proposed causes makes organization and later analysis distinct.')
add(89,'''Imagine a team has a shared Definition of Done for an increment. In this hypothetical standard, required quality measures include passing the agreed checks and completing the agreed review. The exact checks are invented for the exercise; the shared standard must be at least as rigorous as any applicable organizational standard.

Before calling work done, check it against every defined quality criterion and identify unmet criteria. If one required review remains incomplete, the item does not meet the Definition of Done. Under the stated Scrum rule, an item that does not meet it cannot be released or presented at the Sprint Review.

Now compare that with a story’s acceptance condition, such as “Selecting an existing order displays current instructions.” The condition specifies completion for this particular story. Even if the behavior satisfies that condition, it does not replace the shared quality standard. The learner needs to distinguish story-specific behavior from the state required of the increment.

The useful output is a clear check against the shared standard, not a subjective declaration that the work looks finished. Keep unmet criteria visible and do not quietly lower the standard to match what was completed. The BA can help clarify story conditions while the Scrum Team follows the shared Definition of Done.''','''Passing a story’s acceptance criteria does not replace the shared Definition of Done. An item with an unmet required quality criterion cannot be labeled done, released, or presented at Sprint Review under this brief’s rule. Do not reinterpret that as merely optional reporting guidance.''','Preserve the release/review restriction and organizational-standard floor explicitly.')
add(90,'''Suppose a BI model needs to report order amounts by customer and branch. A star schema separates the observations or events from tables that describe business entities.

For this hypothetical model, choose the fact grain as one row per order. OrderFact stores order observations, dimension keys, and a numeric amount. CustomerDim and BranchDim describe their entities and attributes, with unique key columns identifying their rows. Connect those dimensions to the central fact table using one-to-many relationships through each dimension’s unique key.

The grain decision keeps the fact rows at a consistent level of detail. Do not mix one row per order with one row per branch-month total in that same example fact table and then describe all rows as equivalent observations. The dimension tables provide descriptive information, while the fact table holds the chosen observations or events and often numeric measures.

The useful mental model is a central fact table surrounded by descriptive dimensions. Keys connect an observation to the relevant descriptions. The example uses numeric Amount, but the brief says fact tables often have numeric measures; it does not require every fact table to contain the exact same fields or measures.''','''A fact table and a dimension table are not interchangeable because both contain columns. Dimensions describe entities; facts store observations or events at a consistent grain. Mixing detail levels in the example would defeat that grain decision.''','Preserve “often” for numeric measures; do not add unsupported dimensional modeling types.','HIGH','diagram_animation','Arranging dimensions around a fact table and highlighting a consistent row grain makes the star structure clear.')
add(91,'''Imagine a Power BI model with CustomerDim and OrderFact. A visual uses customer descriptions and order amounts, so the model relationship needs to propagate filters between related tables.

Use Modeling > Manage relationships > New and choose the two tables and matching columns. For this hypothetical data, CustomerDim.CustomerID is unique and OrderFact.CustomerID repeats across orders, so set cardinality according to that uniqueness and check the active relationship setting.

Choose cross-filter direction deliberately. Single propagates filters in one direction; Both allows propagation in both directions. These are different choices, not an instruction to select Both whenever a report has more than one table. The direction must be considered for the intended filter behavior.

The useful output is a relationship whose matching columns, cardinality, active setting, and filtering direction are understood. If a report combines fields from both tables, explain how the relationship enables filters to reach related data rather than assuming that similar column names are enough. The example stops at the supported relationship settings and does not invent automatic conflict resolution or a universal recommendation for every model.''','''A relationship is not just a line for display: it propagates filters. Also, Both is not automatically the best setting; it permits two-way propagation and should be chosen deliberately. Check uniqueness and the active setting rather than assuming them.''','No unsupported defaults or claims that bidirectional filtering is universally preferred.','HIGH','screen_demo','Creating a relationship and examining cardinality, active state, and filter direction benefits from tool demonstration.')
add(92,'''Suppose stakeholders need a Power BI page answering “How do order amounts compare across branches?” A report page contains visuals communicating model information. A service dashboard is a separate collection of tiles, so do not use those names as if they described the same artifact.

In report view, select fields from the Data pane and a visual type from Visualizations to add a visual to the canvas. For this hypothetical page, use Branch and Amount for the requested comparison. Arrange the most important information prominently and keep the page focused on that audience question.

Choose comparisons or trends appropriate to the need, avoid clutter, and use consistent display choices. A second visual should have a clear role in answering the question instead of being added only because another chart is available. The example does not prescribe a universal number of visuals.

The useful output here is a focused report page, not a service dashboard created by renaming the canvas. The business question guides field choice and arrangement, while the tool actions explain how a beginner places a visual. Keep service publishing and sharing for their later concept.''','''A Power BI report page and a service dashboard are distinct. Selecting visuals on the report canvas does not by itself create a dashboard collection of tiles. Also, more visuals do not automatically improve the answer; keep the page focused and consistent.''','Explicitly distinguish report page from service dashboard; do not add pinning commands absent from the brief.','HIGH','screen_demo','Selecting fields and visual types and arranging the report canvas is a concrete tool workflow.')
add(93,'''Imagine a Tableau dashboard for comparing order amounts across branches. First build a worksheet view suitable for that comparison. Drag the category dimension Branch to Rows and numeric measure Amount to Columns, or reverse those placements, and use Bar as the mark type. These placements create the category/value bar view for the example.

Select New Dashboard and drag the worksheet from the dashboard pane onto the canvas. A Tableau dashboard combines worksheet views in one place; it is not the same artifact as a single worksheet. Choose a dashboard size suited to how the audience will view it, then arrange the most important view prominently.

Limit the views so the dashboard remains focused and readable. If another worksheet adds a useful perspective, give it a clear role. Do not add every worksheet simply because it exists. The particular layout and size are decisions for the audience, not mandatory measurements in this concept.

The useful output is a dashboard built from appropriate worksheet views, with the core comparison visible. The workflow makes both stages explicit: create the category/value worksheet and then place it on the dashboard. A beginner should not be expected to already know how the first chart comes into existence.''','''A dashboard combines worksheet views; creating a worksheet is not the same as creating the dashboard. Conversely, opening New Dashboard does not create a category/value chart until an appropriate worksheet view is available and placed there.''','Include worksheet bar-chart construction; no invented dashboard size or worksheet count.','HIGH','screen_demo','Creating the worksheet bar chart and then dragging it onto a new dashboard shows both tool stages.')
add(94,'''Suppose a stakeholder dashboard needs to compare branch amounts and show their change over time. Use bars for the category comparison and lines for the time trend. Choose each visual according to the question it serves.

Place the most important information where readers are likely to notice it and keep the layout focused. In this hypothetical page, the branch comparison is the primary question, so give it prominence rather than burying it beneath unrelated views. The exact position is a layout choice for the example, not a universal pixel rule.

Use color consistently and avoid unnecessary variation or decoration that distracts from the data. Reduce unnecessary views and labels so the intended comparison stays readable. Removing a distracting label does not mean removing information needed to interpret the chart; the aim is a clear comparison.

The useful output is a visual design whose chart type, prominence, color, and level of detail support the stakeholder question. Styling choices should not replace the answer. A dashboard full of differently colored charts can still fail to communicate which comparison matters if the layout and views do not remain focused.''','''Decoration is not automatically information. Unnecessary color variety, views, and labels can distract from the intended comparison. But reducing clutter is not a license to remove needed context; preserve readability while keeping important information noticeable.''','No unsupported color prescriptions, fixed layout rules, or chart count limits.','LOW','slides_with_signaling','A simple annotated layout comparison is sufficient to show focus, chart choice, and consistent color.')
add(95,'''Imagine a Power BI Desktop report is ready for the operations audience. Use Home > Publish and choose the destination workspace in the Power BI service. Publishing is the named step for sending the Desktop report to that workspace; the audience still needs appropriate sharing and access.

Select Share for the report or dashboard and specify recipients or an appropriate sharing link. Choose whether recipients can reshare or build on the underlying data, granting only the permissions the audience needs. In this hypothetical case, readers need to consume the report, so do not enable broader permissions merely because the options are available.

Check licensing and capacity conditions. Sharing generally requires Pro or PPU, and free recipients require eligible Premium or Fabric capacity. These are qualified access conditions, not a promise that any recipient with a link can open every shared artifact.

The useful output is a published report shared to its intended audience with considered permissions and access prerequisites. Keep workspace selection, recipient or link selection, permission choices, and license/capacity checks distinct. A successful publish does not establish that each intended recipient satisfies the sharing conditions.''','''A link does not by itself override licensing or capacity requirements. Publishing and sharing also address different steps. Grant reshare or build permissions only when needed, and preserve “generally” in the license statement rather than turning it into an unconditional universal rule.''','Avoid exhaustive licensing advice; retain stated Pro/PPU and eligible capacity qualifications.','HIGH','annotated_ui','Publish, Share, permissions, and access checks are best understood through the corresponding interface actions.')
add(96,'''Suppose OrderFact contains Amount and Quantity. A measure can total Amount while responding to current report filters. Use Modeling > New measure and enter:
TotalAmount = SUM(OrderFact[Amount])
With hypothetical North amounts 40 and 60 and South amount 30, the unfiltered total is 130 and the North-filtered total is 100.

A calculated column instead evaluates an expression for each row. Use New Column and enter:
DoubleQuantity = OrderFact[Quantity] * 2
Rows with quantities 2 and 3 receive 4 and 6. In standard Import mode these values are materialized and refreshed when underlying data is refreshed; do not describe them as the same report-context calculation as a measure.

For the classic date-column prior-year pattern, first use a marked date table with unique, nonblank, gap-free dates spanning full years and the compared periods. Relate it actively to the fact table’s relevant date column so date filters propagate. Then define:
PriorYear = CALCULATE([TotalAmount], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR('Date'[Date]))
This evaluates the base measure with dates shifted one year back. Define growth with:
Growth = DIVIDE([TotalAmount] - [PriorYear], [PriorYear])
and format it as a percentage.

For an example current total of 120 and corresponding prior-year total of 100, growth is (120 − 100) / 100 = 20%. Without an alternate result, DIVIDE returns BLANK when the denominator is zero or blank. Keep the date prerequisites visible: the formula is not a substitute for the marked date table and active relationship required by this pattern.''','''Measures and calculated columns are not two names for the same evaluation. The measure responds to report context; the column calculates each row and, in standard Import mode, materializes values on refresh. For growth, divide the difference by prior year—not current year—and do not omit the date-table prerequisites.''','Keep Import-mode qualification and classic date-column prerequisites; avoid unintroduced DAX functions.','HIGH','annotated_ui','Creating measures and columns, changing filters, and checking date-model prerequisites makes evaluation context and time comparison visible.')
add(97,'''Imagine a Tableau source with Sales and Discount. A calculated field derives new data from existing fields without changing the original source. Use Analysis > Create Calculated Field, name the field Discount Ratio, enter the expression, and select OK:
IIF([Sales] !=0, [Discount]/[Sales],0)

For hypothetical rows with Sales 100 and Discount 10, and Sales 0 and Discount 0, the calculated values are 0.1 and 0. The expression returns Discount divided by Sales when Sales is nonzero, and zero otherwise. It does not establish that a missing value behaves like zero; this example uses known numeric values only.

A basic calculation can operate at row level or at the visualization’s aggregate level, depending on the expression. Numeric calculated fields can be summed automatically when added to a view, so inspect whether that aggregation fits the intended metric. With rows having ratios 0.1 and 0.2, summing the row ratios gives 0.3; do not label that automatically as the intended business discount metric.

The useful output is a calculated field with an understood expression and a deliberate view aggregation. Creating the field and deciding how to summarize it are separate analytical concerns. The example stays with the provided expression rather than adding unsupported aggregate formulas.''','''A calculated field does not modify the original source. Also, a numeric field’s automatic sum is not proof that sum is the appropriate metric. Inspect the expression’s level and choose aggregation for the business question rather than blindly accepting the initial view.''','No unsupported NULL or divide-by-zero claims beyond the IIF result; avoid new aggregation syntax.','HIGH','screen_demo','Creating the field and checking how its numeric values aggregate in a view benefits from a tool demonstration.')
add(98,'''Suppose operations and sales each need a report using the same total-order-amount calculation. A Power BI semantic model contains data-model logic that reports can use. Reusing one published model avoids having each report create separate data and calculation definitions for this shared need.

For this hypothetical arrangement, define the shared business calculation as a measure in the common model. Build both reports from that model when they need the same calculation. One report may focus on branches and another on customers while using the shared calculation definition.

Connect a new report to the existing shared semantic model. Building content on it requires Build permission, so do not assume access to a finished report automatically grants authority to build new content from its model. This concept does not supply a particular connection menu, and the explanation should not invent one.

The useful mental model is shared data-model logic supporting multiple reports, rather than every report independently recreating the same business calculation. The example does not promise every report will display the same result under different filters; it is the common calculation definition that is reused. Keep the distinction between shared model, report, and required Build permission clear.''','''Sharing a calculation definition does not mean every differently filtered report displays an identical number. Reports reuse model logic. Also, building new content on the shared model requires Build permission; do not treat mere report access as that permission.''','Do not invent connection UI steps; distinguish shared definition from filtered result.','MEDIUM','diagram_animation','A model-to-multiple-reports diagram can clarify shared calculations and the separate Build permission requirement.')

